00:00:33.452 --> 00:00:35.660
BUS LEONARD: All right,
thanks, everybody for coming,

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.460
my name is Bus Leonard.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:38.295
CARTER: I'm Carter.

00:00:38.295 --> 00:00:39.170
BRIANNA: I'm Brianna.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.087
BUS LEONARD: And Rosser
is not here right now.

00:00:41.087 --> 00:00:42.890
And our team lead is Ken.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:49.070
And we're working on OJ sealer
for a community in Nicaragua.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:53.030
We were partnered with
the NGO AsoFenix, which

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:59.570
is a organization that does a
rural sustainable development

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:04.280
projects with multiple
communities in Nicaragua.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.350
And they do projects
that promote

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:10.910
clean water, electricity,
income generation, also

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:17.090
educational projects that
help the community be healthy.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:20.240
So our project centers around
the community El Roblar

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.760
that we visited while
we were down there.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:28.370
And they have a large orange
harvest crop that they get.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:32.450
And there's no market for the
oranges during the harvest

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.120
season, because everybody's
harvesting oranges.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.750
So the idea is to
create a way for them

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.990
to sell the oranges or a
product from the oranges

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:44.030
in the offseason
to generate income.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:47.480
And they were introduced
a pasteurization process

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:51.410
to preserve the orange
juice by AsoFenix

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:54.500
and through the
University of Managua.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.330
So that process is in place.

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:59.360
And we are trying to
develop a way for them

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.490
to seal a plastic bag in
order to preserve the juice.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.350
And this is actually
stemming off

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.169
of a project that was started
during last year's D-Lab Energy

00:02:09.169 --> 00:02:10.340
class.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.920
And you see that project
picture at the bottom here.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.740
And our specifications
that we want

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:18.920
to achieve with our
project is to have it--

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:22.460
the device be able to run
off 120 volts of AC current

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:25.010
and while-- and
the seal should be

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:27.230
done while removing
the air from the bag.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:31.820
So there's no air in the bag to
prevent spoiling of the juice

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:35.690
after it's sealed,
before it's sold.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:38.390
And we also want to
create and maintain

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.750
the durable, user friendly
interface that was--

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:45.800
beginning last year with
last year's project.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.300
BRIANNA: So this is a bigger
picture of last year's project.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.700
And it sealed bags
nicely and all,

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.030
but there's several
areas that we

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:57.310
feel need improving on before
it can actually really be used.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.220
The first one is
the power supply.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:05.040
Between last year and this year,
the community we're working in

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.160
got rid of all their 12
volt batteries like that

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.818
because they got a
micro hydro grid.

00:03:10.818 --> 00:03:12.360
And so now they're
running on 120 AC.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.510
So we need to make it actually
be able to run on the power

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.310
that they have.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.470
We mentioned removing all
the air from the bags.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:22.570
The preservation process
isn't very strong.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.020
So it's a key part of making
sure the orange juice stays

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.010
good.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.680
The wide flat surface that
they're sealing on here

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.690
makes it very, very
difficult to do that.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.270
So we wanted to
improve that as well.

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:39.350
And then when we talk
about comfort in using it,

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:45.140
the light that indicated
whether something is hot or not

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.928
was right about here.

00:03:46.928 --> 00:03:48.845
And when you would use
it from the other side,

00:03:48.845 --> 00:03:51.290
it was very difficult to see.

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:56.390
There's a latch that's
really difficult to open.

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:59.570
And trying to get the bags
to get all the air out,

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:01.130
because of the
wide flat surface,

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:02.940
was also very difficult.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:07.510
And the control-- the
timing circuit there,

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:11.090
the used an out of the
box timing circuit.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:17.360
Mainly, I think, because of
limited time that cost $26.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:22.460
So we're also designing a
simplified timing circuit

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:26.300
built out of just components
that is only going

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.940
to be a couple of dollars.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:31.420
CARTER: So we wanted to
first go into experimentation

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:36.910
and see what needed to be done
to get a good seal on a bag.

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:39.580
So we first started off
with what type of plastic

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:40.640
do we need.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:43.932
We basically tested-- we got
some plastic from Nicaragua

00:04:43.932 --> 00:04:44.890
and we brought it back.

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:48.500
And it turned out to
be 25/1000 of an inch.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:52.570
So we're testing with 25/1000 of
an inch and 45/1000 of an inch

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:55.520
because there's
various plastics there.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.360
And so we wanted to test with
multiple types of plastic

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:00.490
to accommodate that.

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:02.950
In addition to that, we wanted
to see what temperature was

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:05.080
needed to fully seal plastic.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:08.870
So we use the standard impulse--
or the industry standard,

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:11.260
which is the impulse
sealer to seal a bag.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:13.900
And we used a thermocouple
to determine the temperature.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:16.300
We got a temperature
of about 195 Celsius.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.420
And you can see, gets
a good seal like this.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.730
Let me pass this
around if you want.

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:24.130
The water should be
orange juice, but--

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:27.070
yeah, so we got 195 Celsius.

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:30.100
That's the temperature
we need to hit.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:32.500
And in addition to
the temperature,

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:34.480
another factor is
pressure, how much

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.330
force do you need to put
down on a nichrome wire

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.530
to get a good seal.

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:43.230
So we use a force plate
sensor and pushed down on it.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:46.370
We got about 56 newtons,
which translates

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:51.130
to about 169 kilopascals
on the nichrome wire.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:53.230
And you could see
over here with--

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:54.970
if you don't apply
enough pressure,

00:05:54.970 --> 00:06:00.948
you get a perforated line,
where there's seals--

00:06:00.948 --> 00:06:02.740
there's a lot of gaps
in between the seals.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.660
So this is not a good
seal, because there

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:09.040
wasn't enough pressure,
although the time was perfect.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.570
For this, with a
6 kilogram weight,

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:14.710
you got a good, solid
seal on the bag.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:15.958
Everything looks good.

00:06:15.958 --> 00:06:16.750
Water did not leak.

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:21.070
And that is ideal.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:22.913
In terms of timing,
we also want to make

00:06:22.913 --> 00:06:25.330
sure you apply the right amount
of time, or else it melts,

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:27.130
the plastic will
melt. So what we

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:28.660
did was we had a
standard weights,

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:34.150
we applied it to our rig and
tested with constant voltage,

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:36.160
different timing--

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:39.670
so we ran the device for
a certain amount of time

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:40.990
and then increased it slowly.

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:42.865
So as you can see, as
we increased more time,

00:06:42.865 --> 00:06:44.860
this was enough-- this
wasn't enough pressure,

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:46.210
because you see the dimples.

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:49.240
But you can see over time, that
is a very small seal to one

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:50.600
that's melted.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:53.680
And so with various weights,
and various voltages,

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.260
and varying the time,
we got figured out

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:59.360
exactly what we need.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:00.940
So in terms of
power and voltage,

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:02.783
I'll pass it off to Brianna.

00:07:02.783 --> 00:07:05.200
BRIANNA: So before I get into
our different tests of power

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:08.530
voltage, I notice that it says
5 volts, 10 volts, 12 volts,

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:11.170
but we were talking
about 120 volts AC.

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:14.680
This is because we decided that
the best way to run something

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:17.800
off the wall power is actually
just to buy an adapter

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:22.180
and plug it in and
convert it to 12 volts DC.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:24.867
Running something off the
wall makes it very difficult

00:07:24.867 --> 00:07:26.200
to have our timing circuit work.

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:31.300
We'd probably have to
rectify that into DC anyway.

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:34.780
120 volts with our nichrome
is like a 4 ohm resistor.

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:38.530
We'd have to add either more
resistors that would do nothing

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:40.900
for the heating and be
expensive because they'd

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:45.130
have to be big power resistors
or lots more nichrome

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:47.560
wire, which would,
again, be expensive.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:51.340
If we built our own adapter,
the price came out to just about

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:55.840
the same as what 12 volt 3, 4
amp adapters are selling for.

00:07:55.840 --> 00:08:00.170
And it would be more complicated
and harder to replace.

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:02.967
So it turns out that the
best solution to running it

00:08:02.967 --> 00:08:05.050
off the wall is just to
buy an adapter running off

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:07.960
the 12 volts anyways.

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:12.280
So we did tests with
power and voltage--

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:14.110
power controlled
by what voltage we

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:18.430
were putting across a
resistor and the time

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:19.902
that we left it on for.

00:08:19.902 --> 00:08:21.610
These are just a couple
of samples there.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:24.250
See at 5 volts, even when we
were doing it for a minute,

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.920
we didn't get hot enough
to melt the bag at all.

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:31.610
I know the pictures aren't
really great, but bear with us.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:34.820
At 10 volts, we had a good
seal in just 20 seconds.

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:40.330
And at 12 volts, by the
time it was 14 seconds,

00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:43.669
we were already
sealing it too much.

00:08:43.669 --> 00:08:45.340
So this was giving
us a good idea

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:48.430
of our trade-offs between
how much power we're

00:08:48.430 --> 00:08:52.300
using, what range of powers
and times we can use.

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:56.830
I think we did get a good
seal at 12 volts at about 8

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:58.480
or 10 seconds.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:00.580
But didn't really have a--

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:03.820
all visible picture
of that to show you.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:06.520
CARTER: So that was the
experimentation that we did.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:10.370
And we wanted to use these
results into our design.

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:12.190
So when we designed
our product, we

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:14.500
wanted to take two things
in the mind, functionality

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:17.020
and usability.

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:20.033
Thing about functionality
is we want the users to be

00:09:20.033 --> 00:09:21.200
looking down on the product.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:23.390
So they can use it on a table.

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:28.280
And they also want
a nice latch system.

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:32.530
So as you can see over here,
we have an angled product.

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:35.518
And since the angle, you can
look down on it, use it easily.

00:09:35.518 --> 00:09:36.310
I should be taller.

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:40.090
But yeah, and with
this angled plane,

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:44.770
you can have a bag
hanging down as such.

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:47.590
So that the liquid
will be in the bag

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:50.632
and that you can get a
good airtight seal here.

00:09:50.632 --> 00:09:52.090
BUS LEONARD: And
then another bonus

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:55.690
is that the excess juice, we're
sealing through juice in order

00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:58.360
to get an airtight seal.

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:00.490
The extra juice, you
can just let run off

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:01.660
after you make the seal.

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:03.368
And you can have a
pot sitting right here

00:10:03.368 --> 00:10:05.890
to catch the excess juice.

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:10.803
So that's another-- and there's
springs in this arm that

00:10:10.803 --> 00:10:12.220
allow you, once
you have-- once we

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:14.350
have a latch system
that will keep

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:16.690
the same force for every seal.

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:18.610
So you'll know that
you have enough force.

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:20.517
And that given the
time and circuit,

00:10:20.517 --> 00:10:22.100
it'll run for the
same amount of time.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:24.760
So the seals will
all be the same.

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:26.725
And then more on
the latch system.

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:32.650
The springs also
provide some give

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:35.320
that will allow this to
compress a little bit more

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:39.430
than the necessary
force for a seal.

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:42.820
So we can just have
a simple rotating

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:45.730
thing out of sheet
metal, something that's

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:48.620
readily available in Nicaragua.

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:51.200
And then you can slide it
over, like the picture.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:53.510
And then when you want it
off, you can press down.

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:56.500
Slide it back up,
you're ready to go.

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:04.690
CARTER: We got some future
things that we need to work on.

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:07.470
One thing would be
combining the ergonomics,

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:09.220
the usability of this
product, in addition

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:10.630
to the functionality.

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:13.390
We were doing a sample test on
a-- basically just two pieces

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:15.800
of wood testing,
putting weight on top.

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:20.640
And so we need to add nichrome
wire onto the base of this

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:23.320
to seal bags with this design.

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:25.870
In addition to that, we need
to finalize the circuitry

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:28.900
and design a housing
device for the electronics

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:32.150
that we would be
placing on the side.

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:34.610
In addition to that, once
we have this completed,

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:36.620
basically just get
users to test this,

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:41.570
make sure it's intuitive, easy
to use, and a great product

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:43.310
overall.

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:46.940
We'd like to thank
Ken, Amy, Amit, D-Lab

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:50.720
overall, and our community
partner AsoFenix.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:51.770
Are there any questions?

00:11:57.392 --> 00:11:59.100
AUDIENCE: Right now,
are you testing that

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:00.570
by sealing through water?

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:01.560
CARTER: Yes.

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:04.210
AUDIENCE: Can you
seal through pulp?

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:05.710
BUS LEONARD: That's
a good question.

00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:07.690
CARTER: We have
yet to test that.

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:09.940
AUDIENCE: It's probably time
to buy some orange juice.

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:14.880
PROFESSOR: Yeah,
I would buy the--

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:17.490
AUDIENCE: The super
extra pulpy version,

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:20.270
throw some sticks
and rocks into it.

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:23.390
PROFESSOR: And at one point,
it would be worth it to test,

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:25.970
like actually squeeze
your own juice,

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:27.530
it's not that hard
to make a bit.

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:37.200
AUDIENCE: How
important is the latch?

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:39.750
Why can't you just hold it?

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:42.240
BUS LEONARD: We wanted
to be able to, I guess,

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:45.960
be easily used by
one person, ideally.

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:51.570
So if they-- we're not
sure that this there

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:54.120
could be cases of
filling like liter bags,

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:56.190
or two liter bags of juice
if they're selling it

00:12:56.190 --> 00:13:00.960
to restaurants for kind of like
wholesale, like to restaurants.

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:05.100
This force pressing down might
not be enough to hold the bag.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:08.422
So they might need to be holding
the bag, pressing a button,

00:13:08.422 --> 00:13:10.380
so you want you want to
be able to do one thing

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:12.120
at a time with your free hand.

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:13.870
CARTER: In addition
to that, you also want

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:15.820
to have a consent
force for each seal.

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:19.140
So there can be variable forces
if you push harder or less

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:20.953
than each time.

00:13:20.953 --> 00:13:22.620
BRIANNA: I'm going
to say that it's also

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:25.615
might be easy for these guys
to remember how much force.

00:13:25.615 --> 00:13:27.240
They can always put
enough force on it.

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:29.550
But when I was
doing some tests, I

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:31.560
wasn't always getting
enough force on there.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:34.350
And the people who are doing
this are women and girls,

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:36.900
so it's--

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:39.150
even like 9, 10,
12-year-old girls,

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.490
being able to latch
it down from having

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:43.430
to hold all the pressure
there all the time.

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:52.162
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] last year.

00:13:52.162 --> 00:13:53.620
BUS LEONARD: No,
this is a new arm.

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:58.600
We left the last year's model
in Nicaragua with AsoFenix.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:00.820
And it works off
the 12 volt battery.

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:06.580
I'm not sure if it's being
used in another community.

00:14:06.580 --> 00:14:09.100
BRIANNA: It's not orange
juice season at the moment.

00:14:09.100 --> 00:14:12.480
So exactly, right now,
it's not being used.

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:14.230
I don't know whether
they will take it out

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:16.498
to the community to use it
when it becomes orange--

00:14:16.498 --> 00:14:18.040
BUS LEONARD: Last
year's project also

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:21.310
did not have the specification
to remove the air.

00:14:21.310 --> 00:14:25.150
So using that while removing
the air is also more difficult.

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:27.596
AUDIENCE: Feedback you have
is based on from the field

00:14:27.596 --> 00:14:28.680
or from your own--

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:30.513
BUS LEONARD: From the
field, from our spring

00:14:30.513 --> 00:14:32.640
break trip down to
Nicaragua and fact

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:35.880
finding there with in El Roblar.

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:38.810
With, yeah.

00:14:38.810 --> 00:14:40.280
AUDIENCE: You have any--

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:42.212
show pictures of that testing?

00:14:42.212 --> 00:14:44.060
BUS LEONARD: Yeah,
we have pictures.

00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:48.780
No, no, thank you.

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:13.390
AUDIENCE: One thing you
might want to think about

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:18.000
is since you're trying to
create at least notionally

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:23.120
sterile stuff, you
might want to start--

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:25.300
it might be time to start
thinking about segregating

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:27.460
this thing into the stuff
you have to keep clean

00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:29.870
in the stuff you don't
have to keep clean.

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:35.510
Because having to reach over
with your sterile hand that's

00:15:35.510 --> 00:15:37.070
in all the juice
and all the stuff

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:39.430
you're trying to be
clean, and reach over

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:41.900
and hit the button on
the top of the timer

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:45.170
is going to become a drag
if that button somehow

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:46.850
has to stay clean,
if you can give them

00:15:46.850 --> 00:15:49.700
things like foot pedals, and
elbow switches, and stuff.

00:15:49.700 --> 00:15:52.310
So that just to think
about the process.

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:54.740
And how you're going to
keep the clean stuff clean

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:57.230
and minimize the amount
of stuff you actually

00:15:57.230 --> 00:15:58.130
have to use sterile.

00:16:02.140 --> 00:16:04.137
AUDIENCE: I probably
missed it, but what

00:16:04.137 --> 00:16:07.225
is the price point [INAUDIBLE].

00:16:12.356 --> 00:16:16.760
BRIANNA: There aren't devices
that use low power like this.

00:16:16.760 --> 00:16:19.640
I'm not sure what the
price for the impulse is,

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:22.340
but running it off batteries
or the micro hydrogrid,

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:23.300
you can't use that.

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:26.210
It uses more power
than-- there's

00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:29.300
a limit to the amount of power
that any device they've run off

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:32.480
the micro hydrogrid that
AsoFenix has installed

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:33.860
is allowed to draw.

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:36.860
BUS LEONARD: So
AsoFenix doesn't allow

00:16:36.860 --> 00:16:41.120
the use of irons, or hair
dryers, or hair straighteners,

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:46.370
or anything that's heavy
power intensive like that.

00:16:46.370 --> 00:16:50.840
BRIANNA: When we compare what
we have to the design that

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:56.990
was made last year, I think
we're probably about $10

00:16:56.990 --> 00:16:59.750
less, which if you
don't include a battery,

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:07.216
I think that makes us around $40
instead of around $50, which--

00:17:07.216 --> 00:17:11.040
AUDIENCE: So it's
not [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:13.950
BRIANNA: No,
instead of sealing--

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:15.609
or instead of the
commercial ones

00:17:15.609 --> 00:17:19.990
that use a large amount of
power for about half a second,

00:17:19.990 --> 00:17:27.609
this will use 30 watts
for more like 10 seconds.

00:17:27.609 --> 00:17:31.300
BUS LEONARD: So
these are our photos

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:35.020
working in the field in the
house we stayed in El Roblar.

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:39.160
A group of about 8 to 10 of
the women of the town came.

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:42.100
And we gave a demonstration
of how it works.

00:17:46.700 --> 00:17:47.420
Scroll through.

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:49.130
So this is-- we
found out that there

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:50.380
were number 12 volt batteries.

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:54.100
So we strung up a bunch
of AA and AAA batteries

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:56.320
just to be able to
do the demonstration.

00:17:56.320 --> 00:17:57.280
So that--

00:17:57.280 --> 00:17:58.990
BRIANNA: We got like
somewhere between 6

00:17:58.990 --> 00:18:02.424
and 10 bags of that
for the batteries.

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:07.705
BUS LEONARD: So
this is the process.

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:18.697
And they actually got to
seal a few bags themselves.

00:18:18.697 --> 00:18:21.030
Let me if you have any questions
as we're going through.

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:28.710
AUDIENCE: Do you have any sense
about a minimally acceptable

00:18:28.710 --> 00:18:29.650
amount of air?

00:18:31.547 --> 00:18:33.380
CARTER: We heard from
the community partners

00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:35.930
that they didn't want any air.

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:42.390
So in terms of room for
air, we're not sure.

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:43.128
We don't know.

00:18:43.128 --> 00:18:44.670
AUDIENCE: Because
I wonder if there's

00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:46.870
a correlation between
the size of that bar

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:50.110
that's hanging off
it and how much air.

00:18:50.110 --> 00:18:52.115
Presumably, if you make
a really sharp edge.

00:18:52.115 --> 00:18:54.490
CARTER: So ideally, we're
going to have the nichrome wire

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:56.230
as close to the edge
as possible to get

00:18:56.230 --> 00:18:58.870
as clean a seal as possible.

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:01.930
Because the more--
the further in it is,

00:19:01.930 --> 00:19:03.560
the more overlap there is.

00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:06.670
So there's going to be
potentially more air.

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:08.360
It's easier to get a--

00:19:08.360 --> 00:19:10.670
clamp like this versus top one.

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:17.980
BUS LEONARD: And that's
actually the last photo.

00:19:17.980 --> 00:19:20.080
We tried to run it
off solar as well.

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:24.010
But this is getting away
from the presentation more.

00:19:24.010 --> 00:19:26.740
AUDIENCE: So how many bags
did you guys seal over

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:28.700
the course of this semester?

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:30.550
BUS LEONARD: Oh,
including the testing,

00:19:30.550 --> 00:19:31.840
where we were just doing--

00:19:34.385 --> 00:19:36.260
AUDIENCE: Five gallons
worth of orange juice,

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:39.260
or five gallons worth
of water, and 100 bags.

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:40.302
CARTER: We usually just--

00:19:40.302 --> 00:19:41.885
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
[? responses, ?]

00:19:41.885 --> 00:19:43.208
or is it just [INAUDIBLE]?

00:19:43.208 --> 00:19:44.500
CARTER: Off every now and then.

00:19:44.500 --> 00:19:50.140
We've been sealing bags the
size of at least 20 or so.

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:53.800
BUS LEONARD: But that was with
our kind of works like model.

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:55.510
So we haven't
combined the sealer

00:19:55.510 --> 00:20:00.220
with this ergonomic
application of it

00:20:00.220 --> 00:20:01.960
yet to have something that.

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:04.290
AUDIENCE: Are those bags--

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:06.920
individual bags largely
available in Nicaragua?

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:08.785
Or do you get it on a long tube?

00:20:08.785 --> 00:20:10.410
BUS LEONARD: You get
it on a long tube.

00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:14.340
And we have about six
feet of that tube here.

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:16.072
AUDIENCE: Have you
thought about how

00:20:16.072 --> 00:20:17.970
that would-- how
that could change

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:19.290
the process a little bit?

00:20:19.290 --> 00:20:21.290
You could fill the whole
tube with orange juice.

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:24.392
CARTER: Seal, seal,
seal, and then just cut.

00:20:24.392 --> 00:20:25.600
BRIANNA: We have a long tube.

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:27.970
And that's for the
individual size.

00:20:27.970 --> 00:20:29.875
We aren't sure that
that would always

00:20:29.875 --> 00:20:31.750
be what they would get,
especially if they're

00:20:31.750 --> 00:20:33.100
like one or two liter bags.

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:34.842
So we wanted something--

00:20:34.842 --> 00:20:37.300
I think we were trying to go
a little more flexible, rather

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:39.780
than specifically for--

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:42.480
that would only be advantaged
with the long tube.

00:20:44.898 --> 00:20:46.815
AUDIENCE: Still use that
process [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:46.815 --> 00:20:51.182
AUDIENCE: Right, and if you
had a little extra add-on

00:20:51.182 --> 00:20:53.158
to support [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:57.604 --> 00:21:02.625
AUDIENCE: That's actually
something [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:02.625 --> 00:21:05.580
all you have filled above
what your sealing another half

00:21:05.580 --> 00:21:07.020
of bag worth of orange juice.

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:08.200
So you don't have to hold--

00:21:08.200 --> 00:21:09.960
If you're filling
one liter bags,

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:12.390
you don't want to have
to hold 50 pounds of bags

00:21:12.390 --> 00:21:13.350
above this thing.

00:21:13.350 --> 00:21:15.780
But if you have it as
a continuous process,

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:17.730
you've only got like
a 1/2 a bag's worth

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:20.610
of stuff supported above, no
matter what size the bag is.

00:21:20.610 --> 00:21:23.670
And then you keep filling it,
once you made that last seal,

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:27.027
then it makes the support
problems a little bit easier.

00:21:27.027 --> 00:21:28.860
You might want to start
thinking about that.

00:21:32.130 --> 00:21:35.550
AUDIENCE: Does the-- once
the orange juice is sealed,

00:21:35.550 --> 00:21:37.326
does it need refrigeration?

00:21:40.077 --> 00:21:41.160
As long as there's no air.

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:42.618
BUS LEONARD:
Pasteurization process

00:21:42.618 --> 00:21:44.880
that comes before, as
long as there's no air.

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:55.667
Is there a standard for size
of bubble that can remain?

00:21:55.667 --> 00:21:57.250
Or does it have to
be absolutely none?

00:22:00.620 --> 00:22:03.828
CARTER: We're not sure.

00:22:03.828 --> 00:22:05.870
PROFESSOR: You guys were
going to look into that.

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:08.200
Any progress there?

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:10.890
BRIANNA: I think I
think most answer we got

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:12.480
was there needs to be removed.

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:14.100
There needs to be no air.

00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:18.660
And whether that's
overstating the requirement

00:22:18.660 --> 00:22:21.960
or actually there
really actually

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:25.230
needs to be no air, that's
where we're unsure, I think.

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:28.373
So we're making our goal be
there be actually no air.

00:22:28.373 --> 00:22:29.790
PROFESSOR: And you
started testing

00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:32.760
to see if you can
actually successfully

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:36.900
seal with zero air, or
zero noticeable air?

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:44.460
BRIANNA: I think we did one with
our flat one that wasn't even

00:22:44.460 --> 00:22:46.380
that we had no air in.

00:22:49.540 --> 00:22:53.683
AUDIENCE: How crazy is there
pasteurization process?

00:22:53.683 --> 00:22:55.480
Put it in a pot, boil
it for 10 minutes,

00:22:55.480 --> 00:22:57.260
and then put in the back?

00:22:57.260 --> 00:22:58.860
BUS LEONARD: Some citric acid.

00:23:04.235 --> 00:23:06.360
AUDIENCE: I'll bet it
depends a lot more on keeping

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:09.120
the clean room clean
than on air in the bag.

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:11.310
Because there's air in the
bottle of Sunny Delight.

00:23:11.310 --> 00:23:13.770
There's air at the top of--

00:23:13.770 --> 00:23:15.930
there's air at the top
of the jar of pickles.

00:23:15.930 --> 00:23:17.670
It's pasteurized.

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:20.600
And it's highly acid.

00:23:20.600 --> 00:23:25.920
And you're trying to [INAUDIBLE]
to give them [INAUDIBLE]..

00:23:31.730 --> 00:23:34.970
PROFESSOR: Well, it'd be good
to both be able to answer that

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:39.320
and also know even if you have
the answer that air is OK,

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:42.740
if they are convinced
that air is not OK,

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:44.790
that may be a knockout blow.

00:23:44.790 --> 00:23:47.600
And so practicing to
make sure the ergonomics

00:23:47.600 --> 00:23:49.880
and whatever your
process are set up such

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:52.940
that you can actually ensure
that most of the time,

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:57.230
you're getting a bag with no air
visible I think would be good

00:23:57.230 --> 00:23:59.090
to practice that a bunch.

00:24:05.540 --> 00:24:11.090
AUDIENCE: I heard you guys talk
about [INAUDIBLE] I'm not sure

00:24:11.090 --> 00:24:12.650
if I missed it,
but are you finding

00:24:12.650 --> 00:24:17.130
the that keeping [INAUDIBLE]
surface [INAUDIBLE]..

00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:21.590
I'm just wondering [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:21.590 --> 00:24:24.920
BRIANNA: It probably wouldn't
be directly wood like that.

00:24:24.920 --> 00:24:26.390
This was an earlier model.

00:24:26.390 --> 00:24:31.310
We've got it covered in
plastic, probably the way

00:24:31.310 --> 00:24:32.390
we're going to go.

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:37.530
Last year was
painted or lacquered.

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:40.106
So definitely be some kind of
more wipe-downable surface.

00:24:47.140 --> 00:24:48.970
AUDIENCE: As long as it--

00:24:48.970 --> 00:24:51.275
what kills wood is going
wet, dry, wet, dry, wet,

00:24:51.275 --> 00:24:52.900
dry over and over
again, like if you're

00:24:52.900 --> 00:24:54.500
going to run it
all day long wet,

00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:55.750
it's not the end of the world.

00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:02.973
You just might not want to
make it out of something--

00:25:02.973 --> 00:25:04.890
it's nice to be able to
make the surface flat.

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:05.807
So you can clean them.

00:25:05.807 --> 00:25:08.860
So really soft [INAUDIBLE]
and harder stuff,

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:12.067
but harder would
repel [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:12.067 --> 00:25:13.900
AUDIENCE: I think one
of their main concerns

00:25:13.900 --> 00:25:16.990
also was capturing
the overflow juice.

00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:19.510
In that case, you want
a very sterile surface.

00:25:19.510 --> 00:25:21.450
And as clean as you can get.

00:25:21.450 --> 00:25:25.330
If you have an extra 8 inches
off your roll of plastic

00:25:25.330 --> 00:25:26.830
or 6 inches off
your roll of plastic

00:25:26.830 --> 00:25:29.830
that you can just wrap that
bottom portion with it as kind

00:25:29.830 --> 00:25:31.000
of runoff.

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:35.160
Then you don't actually have to
worry about cleaning your wood

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:36.610
as much as you would have.

00:25:36.610 --> 00:25:38.250
BRIANNA: It's also
nice in the overflow

00:25:38.250 --> 00:25:41.240
to soak into the device
rather than being reclaimed.

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:47.030
AUDIENCE: Probably
the first [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:50.140 --> 00:25:52.406
PROFESSOR: OK, thanks, you guys.

00:25:52.406 --> 00:25:55.334
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:02.200
VIVIAN: Hi, we're
the charcoal group.

00:26:02.200 --> 00:26:02.975
I'm Vivian.

00:26:02.975 --> 00:26:03.850
JESSICA: I'm Jessica.

00:26:03.850 --> 00:26:05.440
CURT: And I'm Curt.

00:26:05.440 --> 00:26:07.840
VIVIAN: And our project
mentors are Amelia,

00:26:07.840 --> 00:26:12.730
raise your hand, and
Steve over there.

00:26:12.730 --> 00:26:17.300
So first, our main--

00:26:20.790 --> 00:26:26.970
sorry-- OK, our main
motivation for our project

00:26:26.970 --> 00:26:29.970
stems from the work we
did with a Nicaraguan

00:26:29.970 --> 00:26:32.580
based NGO called GrupoFenix
and more specifically, we

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:35.460
worked with a cooperative called
the Mujeres Solares, which

00:26:35.460 --> 00:26:38.100
is the solar wind.

00:26:38.100 --> 00:26:41.220
Basically, we
introduced the idea

00:26:41.220 --> 00:26:43.680
of D-Lab's agri-waste charcoal.

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:45.910
And for those of who
aren't familiar with it,

00:26:45.910 --> 00:26:48.870
it's basically a process
of making charcoal out

00:26:48.870 --> 00:26:51.780
of agri-waste or
biomass, instead of wood.

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:54.420
So you reduce deforestation.

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:57.630
And it burns a lot cleaner.

00:26:57.630 --> 00:27:01.690
We really wanted to
improve this process.

00:27:01.690 --> 00:27:03.810
So that the women would
feel more comfortable

00:27:03.810 --> 00:27:05.700
integrating this into
their everyday lives,

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:10.800
or possibly using this as a
business activity for them.

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:14.750
Right now, there is a general
discomfort with the process.

00:27:14.750 --> 00:27:16.500
And Jess can talk to
you a little bit more

00:27:16.500 --> 00:27:18.052
about those specifics.

00:27:18.052 --> 00:27:20.010
JESSICA: So what was
really unique to our group

00:27:20.010 --> 00:27:23.670
was that we had the language
to get the direct feedback

00:27:23.670 --> 00:27:24.580
from the women.

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:26.610
So we didn't have to go
through a third person.

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:30.450
Instead, I was able to interpret
exactly what they were saying.

00:27:30.450 --> 00:27:35.580
And I have highlighted here
that the biggest issue for them

00:27:35.580 --> 00:27:38.010
today is still
covering the barrel,

00:27:38.010 --> 00:27:41.730
because they cannot bathe
afterwards after dealing with

00:27:41.730 --> 00:27:43.800
so much heat of the barrel.

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:46.050
They say that the heat is
too intensive for them.

00:27:46.050 --> 00:27:48.540
And then would bathe
with cold water,

00:27:48.540 --> 00:27:50.230
and then they would
get sick afterwards.

00:27:50.230 --> 00:27:52.140
So that's the biggest
gap for them right now.

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:56.430
Even when we discussed the
different project ideas

00:27:56.430 --> 00:28:00.030
that we had to make the
charcoal process better,

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:01.980
they still brought
us back to we really

00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:04.680
want you to work on
the barrel to something

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:06.210
so that it doesn't feel so hot.

00:28:12.913 --> 00:28:14.330
VIVIAN: So we're
going to show you

00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:21.020
a video of women
doing a charcoal burn.

00:28:21.020 --> 00:28:22.560
JESSICA: Well, I
can't fast forward.

00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:25.080
This is the third charcoal
burn that the women are

00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:26.250
doing by themselves.

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:27.180
We trained them.

00:28:27.180 --> 00:28:29.730
And then we had them do the
first two by themselves.

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:30.823
They were successful.

00:28:30.823 --> 00:28:31.740
This is the third one.

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:34.680
And you can see that
even in the third trial,

00:28:34.680 --> 00:28:38.190
there's still some
uncoordination

00:28:38.190 --> 00:28:39.990
in the barrel sealing.

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:41.910
This is a current
design, where the barrel

00:28:41.910 --> 00:28:43.200
sits on top of the bricks.

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:47.470
And you have to tilt and kick,
and sometimes kick too much.

00:28:47.470 --> 00:28:50.820
And so what we can see here are
the main points, the main pain

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:54.000
points of the
process, which are--

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:56.700
stop that right now, which
are the heat intensity

00:28:56.700 --> 00:28:58.830
of the process, which is
what they've identified,

00:28:58.830 --> 00:29:00.390
and of course, the safety issue.

00:29:00.390 --> 00:29:02.400
And we realize that we
need to address these two

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:05.070
concerns while still meeting
the same quality of a charcoal

00:29:05.070 --> 00:29:06.820
burn.

00:29:06.820 --> 00:29:12.060
CURT: Great, so in order to meet
these criteria that Jess just

00:29:12.060 --> 00:29:15.660
highlighted, our
design submerges

00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:19.710
the bricks on which the
barrel sits into the ground.

00:29:19.710 --> 00:29:23.125
And so basically,
what we've done,

00:29:23.125 --> 00:29:24.750
here, I can show you
on our model here.

00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:28.950
This is-- one of the
ideas of the charcoal

00:29:28.950 --> 00:29:32.370
burn is that it uses
commonly available materials,

00:29:32.370 --> 00:29:33.630
like a oil drum.

00:29:33.630 --> 00:29:39.060
So I've used a Natty Ice can
here to, in the same spirit.

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:42.510
And so instead of sitting
on top of the ground,

00:29:42.510 --> 00:29:46.290
it now sits on bricks that
are submerged into holes.

00:29:46.290 --> 00:29:50.050
And so the design
is pretty simple.

00:29:50.050 --> 00:29:52.110
There is a dugout whole.

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:55.920
There are six bricks that
line three channels which

00:29:55.920 --> 00:29:57.210
allow for airflow.

00:29:57.210 --> 00:30:01.800
In this space right here,
the ground and the barrel

00:30:01.800 --> 00:30:02.520
form a seal.

00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:09.210
So the barrel does not move
once it's been lit on fire.

00:30:09.210 --> 00:30:12.540
And we've calculated
the airflow.

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:16.350
So that there is sufficient
airflow through those channels

00:30:16.350 --> 00:30:19.290
into the holes that are at
the bottom of the barrel.

00:30:19.290 --> 00:30:24.840
So that the-- while it's
burning, there's enough air.

00:30:24.840 --> 00:30:31.590
And so to work on sealing
these channels, we have--

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:35.950
we've come up with these
metal plates right here.

00:30:35.950 --> 00:30:38.500
So essentially, this
is just sheet metal.

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:42.220
And it has these handles
on top, we call them.

00:30:42.220 --> 00:30:47.490
And they can be pushed
in underneath the--

00:30:47.490 --> 00:30:50.940
over, rather, the three
channels to seal them.

00:30:50.940 --> 00:30:52.720
And just with a
little bit of sand,

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:54.930
it forms a seal around
the whole barrel.

00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:57.360
So eliminates the need
for tilting the barrel,

00:30:57.360 --> 00:30:59.650
kicking it, kicking out the
bricks, and all of that.

00:30:59.650 --> 00:31:02.613
And we experimented
with a number of shapes

00:31:02.613 --> 00:31:03.280
here at the top.

00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:04.380
So this is semi-circular.

00:31:04.380 --> 00:31:07.560
We also have triangular
shapes, and then

00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:09.240
various types of
handles, which Jessica

00:31:09.240 --> 00:31:12.340
we'll go into in a second.

00:31:12.340 --> 00:31:16.470
So an overview of the benefits
of this design compared

00:31:16.470 --> 00:31:19.110
to the existing--

00:31:19.110 --> 00:31:21.180
the existing portion
of the process

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:22.980
that we're trying to
fit are first that it

00:31:22.980 --> 00:31:25.500
minimizes the heat exposure.

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:29.820
Because the flaming barrel
is now in the ground,

00:31:29.820 --> 00:31:32.910
and there's no space
for the-- there's

00:31:32.910 --> 00:31:35.290
minimized space for
this heat to come out.

00:31:35.290 --> 00:31:40.500
The second is that one person
can seal the barrel, rather

00:31:40.500 --> 00:31:41.550
than needing two.

00:31:41.550 --> 00:31:44.505
And we can show video
of that right now.

00:31:47.470 --> 00:31:55.490
Yeah, so this is
the entire process.

00:31:55.490 --> 00:31:57.430
And I'll talk through this.

00:31:57.430 --> 00:32:01.060
Third is that you can
still light the barrel

00:32:01.060 --> 00:32:02.680
through the channels.

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:08.260
And the barrel doesn't move
once it's on fire and flaming.

00:32:08.260 --> 00:32:10.690
So as you can see,
all Jessica is

00:32:10.690 --> 00:32:17.470
doing here is just pushing the
plates in at a safe distance

00:32:17.470 --> 00:32:23.592
and with minimal exposure
to heat basically.

00:32:30.350 --> 00:32:31.900
JESSICA: And so
from our experiment,

00:32:31.900 --> 00:32:38.500
we were able to conclude what
designs of sheet and handles

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:40.990
were better equipped to
meet our project attributes.

00:32:40.990 --> 00:32:43.090
We wanted to be able
to steer the sheet.

00:32:43.090 --> 00:32:46.570
And the better design for that
was the semi-circular plate.

00:32:46.570 --> 00:32:48.760
In terms of
maneuvering and being

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:50.650
precise about where
the sheet was going,

00:32:50.650 --> 00:32:54.460
the lipped handle, which
is this one right here,

00:32:54.460 --> 00:32:58.060
allowed for the wood to come
in and kind of fit right there

00:32:58.060 --> 00:33:00.820
just enough to be able to
maneuver the sheet into where

00:33:00.820 --> 00:33:03.160
we wanted it.

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:04.780
The lifting
potential of the lift

00:33:04.780 --> 00:33:07.510
handle allowed us
to kick the sheet up

00:33:07.510 --> 00:33:11.260
a little bit if it ever got
stuck right before the brick.

00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:13.540
And the three sheets
worked equally

00:33:13.540 --> 00:33:16.600
as well to diminish
the heat exposure

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:21.470
and to lessen the time that
it took to seal the barrel.

00:33:21.470 --> 00:33:24.070
Now, stepping back for a second,
the sheet metal is only one

00:33:24.070 --> 00:33:26.860
of three sealing--

00:33:26.860 --> 00:33:29.500
barrel sealing alternatives that
we're considering right now.

00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:31.900
The other two are
inserting a break

00:33:31.900 --> 00:33:35.590
into the channel of the
dugout and covering the rest

00:33:35.590 --> 00:33:36.310
with sand.

00:33:36.310 --> 00:33:38.470
And the third is even
simpler than that,

00:33:38.470 --> 00:33:42.730
just shoveling in or pouring
in sand into the three holes.

00:33:42.730 --> 00:33:46.570
And of course, on the left,
we have different design--

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:49.420
excuse me, specifications
that we want this to meet.

00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:52.030
Because it's part of the
process that before was most

00:33:52.030 --> 00:33:53.590
heat intensive.

00:33:53.590 --> 00:33:55.720
You'll see, there's an
even spread right now

00:33:55.720 --> 00:33:58.180
of all the three designs.

00:33:58.180 --> 00:34:00.970
For example, the sheet metal
is best in diminishing,

00:34:00.970 --> 00:34:03.190
or in lessening the time
that it takes to seal.

00:34:03.190 --> 00:34:06.100
But the sand could be
done from farthest away.

00:34:06.100 --> 00:34:10.239
You just sort of take the sand
and throw it into the barrel,

00:34:10.239 --> 00:34:11.469
throw it in along the side.

00:34:11.469 --> 00:34:14.530
But this is due to the
metrics of the attributes.

00:34:14.530 --> 00:34:16.389
For example, the
labor intensity,

00:34:16.389 --> 00:34:19.870
we consider that it's
easier to seal the hole.

00:34:19.870 --> 00:34:21.969
But you do have to
spend some time finding

00:34:21.969 --> 00:34:24.730
the sheet metal, which is
readily available in Nicaragua.

00:34:24.730 --> 00:34:28.630
But you still have to make and
manufacture the sheet metal

00:34:28.630 --> 00:34:33.219
with the handles,
only once though.

00:34:33.219 --> 00:34:35.080
VIVIAN: So in our
experiment, we concluded

00:34:35.080 --> 00:34:40.150
that our dugout was successful
in addressing the need

00:34:40.150 --> 00:34:42.699
to reduce heat exposure.

00:34:42.699 --> 00:34:45.010
It channels all the heat
down into the dugout

00:34:45.010 --> 00:34:49.159
instead of at the
people working on it.

00:34:49.159 --> 00:34:53.230
We also were able to decrease a
safety risk of the tip and kick

00:34:53.230 --> 00:34:54.969
method.

00:34:54.969 --> 00:34:59.590
Our sheet metal seals were also
successful in creating a seal

00:34:59.590 --> 00:35:02.350
and resulting in a quality burn.

00:35:02.350 --> 00:35:04.690
We did get some really good
carbonized material out

00:35:04.690 --> 00:35:06.670
of this burn.

00:35:06.670 --> 00:35:09.910
But there are definitely
still improvements to be made.

00:35:09.910 --> 00:35:13.510
We can still work on the
maneuverability and handling

00:35:13.510 --> 00:35:16.510
of these seals with the handles.

00:35:16.510 --> 00:35:19.960
And we can also work on
adding a little more distance

00:35:19.960 --> 00:35:23.060
between the person
and the barrel.

00:35:23.060 --> 00:35:25.690
JESSICA: And so we're
at our next steps, which

00:35:25.690 --> 00:35:27.910
are going to be to
send the documented

00:35:27.910 --> 00:35:31.420
process of our new barrel
design or charcoal making design

00:35:31.420 --> 00:35:32.093
to the women.

00:35:32.093 --> 00:35:34.510
And they're going to make a
charcoal burn early next week.

00:35:34.510 --> 00:35:36.957
We're going to phone call them
and talk to them directly,

00:35:36.957 --> 00:35:39.040
I'll talk to them directly,
and get their feedback

00:35:39.040 --> 00:35:44.380
on what the heat experience
was and see how they can--

00:35:44.380 --> 00:35:47.530
explain that and whether
it's better or worse.

00:35:47.530 --> 00:35:49.030
And finally, we're
going to continue

00:35:49.030 --> 00:35:51.460
to look at improving
the sheet metal design

00:35:51.460 --> 00:35:54.130
and seeing how we can
manufacture it easier,

00:35:54.130 --> 00:35:56.440
or how we can just
get it a better design

00:35:56.440 --> 00:35:58.430
so we can maneuver it better.

00:35:58.430 --> 00:36:01.070
And finally, of course, we're
here to incorporate your input.

00:36:01.070 --> 00:36:02.620
So questions?

00:36:06.940 --> 00:36:08.250
Questions?

00:36:08.250 --> 00:36:09.030
Yes, Benjamin.

00:36:09.030 --> 00:36:11.580
AUDIENCE: Have been able to
talk to them about the three

00:36:11.580 --> 00:36:15.390
different options of how to
seal the holes in terms of

00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:18.400
would you rather make a
plate, or would you rather

00:36:18.400 --> 00:36:20.710
[INAUDIBLE].

00:36:20.710 --> 00:36:23.890
JESSICA: So no, the
answer is no, not about

00:36:23.890 --> 00:36:26.200
those three specific designs.

00:36:26.200 --> 00:36:28.600
But in our conversations
with Lindsay,

00:36:28.600 --> 00:36:31.457
which is sort of their
project coordinator or--

00:36:31.457 --> 00:36:32.290
VIVIAN: Our liaison.

00:36:32.290 --> 00:36:33.165
JESSICA: Our liaison.

00:36:33.165 --> 00:36:34.840
Our liaison the
community, she says

00:36:34.840 --> 00:36:37.150
that-- she continues to
say that the heat of it

00:36:37.150 --> 00:36:38.840
is the biggest exposure.

00:36:38.840 --> 00:36:41.330
So rather than have them
do the test trials and all

00:36:41.330 --> 00:36:44.500
the experimentation of like
which feels best or taking all

00:36:44.500 --> 00:36:45.935
this data that
we've been taking,

00:36:45.935 --> 00:36:47.560
we tried to get the
data first and then

00:36:47.560 --> 00:36:50.325
submit our conclusive
recommendations to them.

00:36:50.325 --> 00:36:52.450
And then that's what they're
going to do this week.

00:36:55.820 --> 00:36:58.465
AUDIENCE: How are you actually
going to communicate to them?

00:36:58.465 --> 00:37:02.510
Do you need to describe
the whole thing as well?

00:37:02.510 --> 00:37:05.660
JESSICA: Yeah, right now, when
you train a community to make

00:37:05.660 --> 00:37:09.410
a charcoal burn, there's a
nifty document with diagrams

00:37:09.410 --> 00:37:11.390
that D-Lab has produced
that tells them

00:37:11.390 --> 00:37:14.150
how to make the holes, or
how to set up the bricks,

00:37:14.150 --> 00:37:15.360
and all the process.

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:16.670
And so we're going to do that.

00:37:16.670 --> 00:37:17.970
They've already been
trained with that.

00:37:17.970 --> 00:37:20.360
And they have that document, we
trained them over spring break.

00:37:20.360 --> 00:37:22.818
And so we're just going to sort
of an addendum, an appendix

00:37:22.818 --> 00:37:24.372
to what the new
process should be,

00:37:24.372 --> 00:37:26.330
and how they should dig
the hole, and all that.

00:37:26.330 --> 00:37:28.830
So they can do their
charcoal burn next week.

00:37:28.830 --> 00:37:31.760
CURT: And we have all the
specifications for the hole

00:37:31.760 --> 00:37:33.675
based on air flow, so that's--

00:37:33.675 --> 00:37:35.300
VIVIAN: How wide the
channel should be.

00:37:35.300 --> 00:37:39.160
CURT: Exactly, so we can
send them very, very exact--

00:37:39.160 --> 00:37:41.510
AUDIENCE: So before with
the original document,

00:37:41.510 --> 00:37:44.513
they were able to go through
it without any training?

00:37:44.513 --> 00:37:46.680
JESSICA: No, we were there
to train them personally.

00:37:46.680 --> 00:37:49.590
You can-- it's hard to
communicate just with

00:37:49.590 --> 00:37:51.463
a document of step
by step instructions,

00:37:51.463 --> 00:37:53.130
even though they're
really well drafted,

00:37:53.130 --> 00:37:55.107
because they still
have questions on--

00:37:55.107 --> 00:37:57.190
it's really about understanding
the why of things.

00:37:57.190 --> 00:37:59.148
But now that they understand
the why of things,

00:37:59.148 --> 00:38:01.180
like why it has to be
sealed at a certain time,

00:38:01.180 --> 00:38:03.660
now it's a matter of just
saying, instead of mounting

00:38:03.660 --> 00:38:06.390
the barrel on the bricks,
instead make a hole,

00:38:06.390 --> 00:38:09.890
insert bricks, and
put the barrel on top.

00:38:09.890 --> 00:38:11.890
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
expectations [INAUDIBLE]..

00:38:17.878 --> 00:38:18.920
AUDIENCE: It seems like--

00:38:18.920 --> 00:38:20.420
how do you-- if
you're going to plug

00:38:20.420 --> 00:38:26.350
the hole with a third brick,
how do you get the brick there?

00:38:26.350 --> 00:38:28.090
I mean that, does
that inevitably

00:38:28.090 --> 00:38:30.340
involve your hand a half an
inch away from the barrel?

00:38:30.340 --> 00:38:32.840
JESSICA: Sure, so you can make
other contraptions that we've

00:38:32.840 --> 00:38:34.735
considered, like a reach,
the things that you

00:38:34.735 --> 00:38:37.360
use to reach really far away and
just grab it and put it there.

00:38:37.360 --> 00:38:42.082
But the simplest
design is that when--

00:38:42.082 --> 00:38:43.540
I'll show you the
picture, when you

00:38:43.540 --> 00:38:45.670
can walk right
next to the barrel,

00:38:45.670 --> 00:38:48.160
you can stand right here.

00:38:48.160 --> 00:38:49.660
And you're not feeling any heat.

00:38:49.660 --> 00:38:51.370
So you can-- you just
drop in the brick.

00:38:51.370 --> 00:38:53.590
You're technically not
feeling any heat over you.

00:38:53.590 --> 00:38:55.930
Whereas, even with the
sheet metal design,

00:38:55.930 --> 00:38:58.060
you're directly in front
of the channel that does

00:38:58.060 --> 00:39:01.650
have the heat coming out of
it, if that makes any sense.

00:39:01.650 --> 00:39:03.350
AUDIENCE: I'm just
surprised that you're

00:39:03.350 --> 00:39:07.250
getting so heat in the
channel [INAUDIBLE]..

00:39:07.250 --> 00:39:08.330
JESSICA: Comparatively--

00:39:08.330 --> 00:39:11.072
CURT: I think a lot
of it is smoke, too.

00:39:11.072 --> 00:39:12.780
A lot of smoke comes
out of the channels.

00:39:12.780 --> 00:39:14.190
JESSICA: And comparatively,
remember that it

00:39:14.190 --> 00:39:15.270
was all on bricks before.

00:39:15.270 --> 00:39:17.700
So all the heat was
radiating out of the bottom.

00:39:17.700 --> 00:39:20.280
And that was going right to
their feet, to their ankles,

00:39:20.280 --> 00:39:21.780
and whatnot.

00:39:21.780 --> 00:39:25.860
AUDIENCE: But there's-- if it's
just as easy for them to bring

00:39:25.860 --> 00:39:29.910
nine bricks as six bricks, then
the easiest way to tell them

00:39:29.910 --> 00:39:34.060
how wide the hole should be is
that it should be a brick wide.

00:39:34.060 --> 00:39:38.210
Just measure it with the bricks
you're about to use to plug in.

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:43.075
But you can't get there
[INAUDIBLE] is not working.

00:39:43.075 --> 00:39:44.700
CURT: So that's why
for the time being,

00:39:44.700 --> 00:39:48.050
we still prefer a solution
with the metal plates.

00:39:48.050 --> 00:39:51.670
PROFESSOR: So Jess, you
tested with a hot barrel

00:39:51.670 --> 00:39:53.500
putting the bricks in?

00:39:53.500 --> 00:39:55.120
Not with the hot barrel, no.

00:39:55.120 --> 00:39:56.500
CURT: We time tested.

00:39:56.500 --> 00:39:58.150
PROFESSOR: OK,
because I question

00:39:58.150 --> 00:40:01.465
that you wouldn't be exposed
to heat but putting a brick in.

00:40:01.465 --> 00:40:03.790
JESSICA: In our video,
I was significantly

00:40:03.790 --> 00:40:06.970
close to the sheet metal.

00:40:06.970 --> 00:40:08.885
And I had to be
over the channel.

00:40:08.885 --> 00:40:11.260
So I can only imagine that
being on the side of it, where

00:40:11.260 --> 00:40:13.558
the smoke isn't rising
would be better.

00:40:13.558 --> 00:40:15.100
But that is actually
what we're going

00:40:15.100 --> 00:40:17.680
to be testing on Monday, which
is we're going to have two--

00:40:17.680 --> 00:40:18.940
