00:52:19.020 --> 00:52:20.910
three of these
cells in series, you

00:52:20.910 --> 00:52:24.245
could go up to 1.5 volts, which
is about a normal pencil cell.

00:52:24.245 --> 00:52:25.620
And then we thought
we would just

00:52:25.620 --> 00:52:29.310
step up that voltage to 5 volts,
which is what a cell phone

00:52:29.310 --> 00:52:31.050
charger uses.

00:52:31.050 --> 00:52:33.432
To do that, we bought--
so this is the circuitry

00:52:33.432 --> 00:52:34.140
that we're using.

00:52:37.450 --> 00:52:40.680
So essentially, a lot of--

00:52:40.680 --> 00:52:44.280
I mean, we couldn't
actually make the step up.

00:52:44.280 --> 00:52:47.400
We tried, but we failed.

00:52:47.400 --> 00:52:50.320
So we decided to just
buy it off the shelf.

00:52:50.320 --> 00:52:53.070
And so this is how
our bread board looks.

00:52:53.070 --> 00:52:56.580
Essentially, this would
be our input right here.

00:52:56.580 --> 00:52:58.980
And this would be 1.5 DC input.

00:52:58.980 --> 00:53:03.180
And then you'd have a 5 volt
DC output coming in from here.

00:53:03.180 --> 00:53:07.020
And basically, your solar
cells would be connected.

00:53:07.020 --> 00:53:09.480
So you're solar cells are here.

00:53:09.480 --> 00:53:14.400
And this little thing fits in
to this depression right here.

00:53:14.400 --> 00:53:16.920
And then so your panels
are connected in series.

00:53:16.920 --> 00:53:22.020
You have two wires connecting
to these terminals.

00:53:22.020 --> 00:53:26.970
It steps up to 5 volts and goes
out to connect to this thing.

00:53:26.970 --> 00:53:29.910
This thing then goes into
your Nokia/whatever phone

00:53:29.910 --> 00:53:34.350
you have and then
charges your phone.

00:53:34.350 --> 00:53:36.045
Do you have the video?

00:53:36.045 --> 00:53:37.920
LIZ DE REGT: Make sure
my volumes on, though.

00:53:46.357 --> 00:53:47.940
JUAN DIAZ: So we
kind of made a video.

00:53:51.198 --> 00:53:53.170
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:53:53.170 --> 00:53:55.750
- OK, so we're
switching on the light.

00:53:55.750 --> 00:53:59.950
[INAUDIBLE] right here.

00:53:59.950 --> 00:54:02.382
Now, we're going to
measure the voltage output

00:54:02.382 --> 00:54:04.900
of our solar cells.

00:54:04.900 --> 00:54:08.290
So 1.6 volts, that's good.

00:54:08.290 --> 00:54:13.118
Now going to measure the
voltage output after we step up.

00:54:13.118 --> 00:54:14.910
LIZ DE REGT: Can you
guys hear that at all?

00:54:14.910 --> 00:54:17.040
- That's good.

00:54:17.040 --> 00:54:19.681
Now Juan's going to
plug in the cellphone.

00:54:22.630 --> 00:54:23.970
And it says charging.

00:54:28.420 --> 00:54:30.372
0.8--

00:54:30.372 --> 00:54:32.580
LIZ DE REGT: Which is the
voltage across the battery.

00:54:32.580 --> 00:54:33.380
So it makes sense
that we're just

00:54:33.380 --> 00:54:35.300
plugging in it's 0.8
volts instead of 0.5

00:54:35.300 --> 00:54:36.060
[END PLAYBACK]

00:54:36.060 --> 00:54:40.430
SID PAL: Yeah, so that's
basically the works like.

00:54:40.430 --> 00:54:42.380
Yeah, I think
that's pretty much--

00:54:42.380 --> 00:54:44.720
LIZ DE REGT: Yeah,
and then we've also

00:54:44.720 --> 00:54:46.770
been talking to the community.

00:54:46.770 --> 00:54:48.830
I'm hoping we can talk to
them on the phone soon.

00:54:48.830 --> 00:54:50.788
This week, they haven't
really been in contact,

00:54:50.788 --> 00:54:52.310
because it's Holy Week.

00:54:52.310 --> 00:54:55.250
But we sent them this in Spanish
and some other instructions

00:54:55.250 --> 00:54:56.820
about what we've
been working on.

00:54:56.820 --> 00:54:59.690
So we kind of hope to
get their feedback.

00:54:59.690 --> 00:55:02.780
This is our-- kind of looks
like model you saw tested,

00:55:02.780 --> 00:55:06.140
kind of can put this stuff
into this indentation here.

00:55:06.140 --> 00:55:09.110
So that when it's not in
use, or when it's noon,

00:55:09.110 --> 00:55:10.520
it can be completely flat.

00:55:10.520 --> 00:55:16.400
This also protects all of
the circuitry from the rain

00:55:16.400 --> 00:55:18.210
and that kind of thing.

00:55:18.210 --> 00:55:20.870
There could be notches
added to the side.

00:55:20.870 --> 00:55:24.260
So that for instance, you could
make it lower by lowering these

00:55:24.260 --> 00:55:27.195
and it could be like
that and stay still.

00:55:27.195 --> 00:55:29.570
And it's also just something
they can really simply make.

00:55:29.570 --> 00:55:32.300
They also make other things
with wood in the same center.

00:55:32.300 --> 00:55:34.520
So they have all the materials.

00:55:34.520 --> 00:55:36.090
It's pretty low cost.

00:55:36.090 --> 00:55:38.165
And you can add--

00:55:38.165 --> 00:55:40.970
WE have a hole
here for security,

00:55:40.970 --> 00:55:42.810
you could see in the picture.

00:55:42.810 --> 00:55:44.900
So that if they want to
tie it up and lock it

00:55:44.900 --> 00:55:48.170
to something while it's
charging, they can do that.

00:55:48.170 --> 00:55:50.630
It's pretty portable, but
it's also not so small

00:55:50.630 --> 00:55:52.505
that's going to just
stick it in their pocket

00:55:52.505 --> 00:55:54.990
and run away with it.

00:55:54.990 --> 00:55:56.050
It's adjustable.

00:55:56.050 --> 00:55:57.590
And just easy for them to make.

00:55:57.590 --> 00:55:59.960
So we're hoping
that they like it.

00:55:59.960 --> 00:56:02.698
They can easily change the
looks like design as well.

00:56:02.698 --> 00:56:04.240
Most of how we're
trying to help them

00:56:04.240 --> 00:56:06.573
is actually, because they're
pretty capable of coming up

00:56:06.573 --> 00:56:07.990
with things like
this themselves,

00:56:07.990 --> 00:56:10.910
but this is just
kind of a suggestion.

00:56:10.910 --> 00:56:13.010
We've also been talking
with some other people who

00:56:13.010 --> 00:56:16.010
are working on the other
aspects of the solar panels,

00:56:16.010 --> 00:56:18.700
like trying to make
the application

00:56:18.700 --> 00:56:20.700
of the glass on top easier
and things like that.

00:56:20.700 --> 00:56:22.380
So other people have
that part covered.

00:56:22.380 --> 00:56:25.040
So we're focusing a lot on
just making it actually able

00:56:25.040 --> 00:56:27.030
to charge a cell phone.

00:56:27.030 --> 00:56:30.630
And what we're looking
to do next is right now,

00:56:30.630 --> 00:56:31.770
it's on the breadboard.

00:56:31.770 --> 00:56:34.098
And it's-- definitely if you
handed this to a customer,

00:56:34.098 --> 00:56:36.640
and we're like don't break it,
something's going to come out.

00:56:36.640 --> 00:56:38.473
And then they won't
know what to do with it.

00:56:38.473 --> 00:56:42.960
So we have to make it more
able to be actually given

00:56:42.960 --> 00:56:47.040
to somebody and not break,
so more durable, I guess.

00:56:47.040 --> 00:56:50.517
We need to be able to
make it easy to explain.

00:56:50.517 --> 00:56:52.350
Because we might not
have anybody down there

00:56:52.350 --> 00:56:54.820
showing them, they can
still learn how to do it.

00:56:54.820 --> 00:56:56.250
They have a mechanic--

00:56:56.250 --> 00:56:58.200
I mean, an engineer
who's been trained

00:56:58.200 --> 00:57:00.820
and should know how to put
this together reasonably well.

00:57:00.820 --> 00:57:03.210
And he's definitely better
at soldering than we are.

00:57:03.210 --> 00:57:06.880
So it should be doable.

00:57:06.880 --> 00:57:07.570
Thank you.

00:57:07.570 --> 00:57:10.840
SID PAL: And I'd like to be
clear, this is just a model.

00:57:10.840 --> 00:57:13.010
This is actually going
to be a solar panel.

00:57:13.010 --> 00:57:16.380
This is not wood, in case
people were kind of confused.

00:57:16.380 --> 00:57:17.380
This is the solar panel.

00:57:17.380 --> 00:57:20.650
But since our project focuses
on converting the voltage

00:57:20.650 --> 00:57:22.450
to a cell phone
charging voltage,

00:57:22.450 --> 00:57:25.180
we decided to just like go
with this just to show you guys

00:57:25.180 --> 00:57:27.650
how it's going to look
when the solar panel is,

00:57:27.650 --> 00:57:29.320
in fact, installed.

00:57:29.320 --> 00:57:31.940
Yeah, that's pretty much it.

00:57:31.940 --> 00:57:33.970
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:57:33.970 --> 00:57:35.380
SID PAL: Three, three.

00:57:35.380 --> 00:57:38.020
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:57:40.335 --> 00:57:41.460
SID PAL: Yeah, pretty much.

00:57:44.000 --> 00:57:47.000
AUDIENCE: What's the
cost of your circuitry?

00:57:47.000 --> 00:57:51.440
SID PAL: So we
estimate-- so right now,

00:57:51.440 --> 00:57:52.670
we bought five of each.

00:57:52.670 --> 00:57:57.200
If you buy them large
scale, sorry, wholesale,

00:57:57.200 --> 00:57:57.830
I think this--

00:57:57.830 --> 00:58:01.370
I think this thing should
cost about $4, this entire--

00:58:01.370 --> 00:58:02.953
LIZ DE REGT: This
cost us like $6.

00:58:02.953 --> 00:58:04.370
SID PAL: This was
about $6 for us,

00:58:04.370 --> 00:58:06.140
but I think it should cost $4.

00:58:06.140 --> 00:58:09.740
And then so the connectors
are actually more expensive,

00:58:09.740 --> 00:58:11.478
but that's because--

00:58:11.478 --> 00:58:12.770
so there are two ways to do it.

00:58:12.770 --> 00:58:14.420
Either you buy the
whole thing, cut it off,

00:58:14.420 --> 00:58:16.045
but that doesn't make
any sense, right?

00:58:16.045 --> 00:58:19.850
So I look-- when we looked it
up online, you can buy about 10

00:58:19.850 --> 00:58:23.990
of them for $7, but
there are probably

00:58:23.990 --> 00:58:26.060
cheaper ways to buy them.

00:58:26.060 --> 00:58:29.000
Maybe if we contacted the
company, asked them about it.

00:58:29.000 --> 00:58:32.212
We're still deciding how
to get them for cheap.

00:58:32.212 --> 00:58:33.170
But we can definitely--

00:58:33.170 --> 00:58:37.520
I think this should be about
$1 max, if you just buy it.

00:58:37.520 --> 00:58:39.555
LIZ DE REGT: And then
when you add in their

00:58:39.555 --> 00:58:40.430
the time they'll use.

00:58:40.430 --> 00:58:42.500
It probably will take them
about eight hours now,

00:58:42.500 --> 00:58:44.042
because they won't
have to cut cells.

00:58:44.042 --> 00:58:49.340
So that'll eat out
another $4 or something,

00:58:49.340 --> 00:58:51.890
because they're getting paid
about $0.50 an hour, I think.

00:58:51.890 --> 00:58:55.260
So it'll definitely be able to
compete with the current one no

00:58:55.260 --> 00:58:55.760
problem.

00:59:00.010 --> 00:59:00.885
SID PAL: Anything--

00:59:00.885 --> 00:59:03.310
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:59:09.540 --> 00:59:10.415
JUAN DIAZ: I'm sorry.

00:59:10.415 --> 00:59:12.366
SID PAL: I'm sorry,
I didn't catch that.

00:59:12.366 --> 00:59:15.465
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:59:18.320 --> 00:59:22.990
SID PAL: So basically
the concept--

00:59:22.990 --> 00:59:25.490
the reason we decided to put
it in the depression underneath

00:59:25.490 --> 00:59:27.830
was that if it did
rain, it wouldn't

00:59:27.830 --> 00:59:29.510
be immediately affected.

00:59:29.510 --> 00:59:31.280
That being said,
this isn't going

00:59:31.280 --> 00:59:33.920
to protect it against-- if you
like leave it out in the rain,

00:59:33.920 --> 00:59:36.120
it's not going to be protected.

00:59:36.120 --> 00:59:38.690
So I guess the concept
is that you leave

00:59:38.690 --> 00:59:40.310
it out only when it's bright.

00:59:40.310 --> 00:59:42.020
But if it suddenly
starts to rain,

00:59:42.020 --> 00:59:45.410
it'll give you a certain
amount of protection against it

00:59:45.410 --> 00:59:47.270
before you can take it back in.

00:59:47.270 --> 00:59:50.178
LIZ DE REGT: And the solar
cells themselves, to be clear,

00:59:50.178 --> 00:59:51.470
are not going to be just like--

00:59:51.470 --> 00:59:52.670
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:59:52.670 --> 00:59:53.780
SID PAL: Just a panel.

00:59:53.780 --> 00:59:57.290
LIZ DE REGT: They'll still do
the process with a silicone

00:59:57.290 --> 00:59:59.810
glass on top, because
that's necessary.

00:59:59.810 --> 01:00:02.900
You can't-- I mean, the
cells get damaged if you

01:00:02.900 --> 01:00:05.150
don't encapsulate them.

01:00:05.150 --> 01:00:06.680
SID PAL: I mean,
just to be clear,

01:00:06.680 --> 01:00:09.212
I realized we didn't
explain how the panel looks.

01:00:09.212 --> 01:00:10.670
The way the panel
looks is that you

01:00:10.670 --> 01:00:13.670
have the three solar cells
in series, which are then

01:00:13.670 --> 01:00:16.130
encapsulated in silicone.

01:00:16.130 --> 01:00:18.320
You have an aluminum frame.

01:00:18.320 --> 01:00:22.400
So you have an aluminum frame,
you have these cells in series.

01:00:22.400 --> 01:00:25.070
And they're encapsulated
by silicone.

01:00:25.070 --> 01:00:27.770
You then pressure--
you put pressure

01:00:27.770 --> 01:00:32.540
on a glass frame on top
of it and leave it sit.

01:00:32.540 --> 01:00:34.950
And then you have a
plastic sheet underneath.

01:00:34.950 --> 01:00:37.700
So this is basically waterproof.

01:00:37.700 --> 01:00:40.057
And this is what we're
talking about will be here.

01:00:40.057 --> 01:00:42.140
LIZ DE REGT: Because these
are the kinds of panels

01:00:42.140 --> 01:00:44.593
that they stick on their
roofs and they use for power.

01:00:44.593 --> 01:00:46.010
SID PAL: Except
in our case, you'd

01:00:46.010 --> 01:00:48.298
have only three
cells as opposed to--

01:00:48.298 --> 01:00:50.090
LIZ DE REGT: Yeah, so
it's a lot less time.

01:00:50.090 --> 01:00:51.740
A lot of the problems
with the big ones

01:00:51.740 --> 01:00:53.703
is when you encapsulate
it, you have

01:00:53.703 --> 01:00:56.120
to push it down and make sure
that there's no air bubbles.

01:00:56.120 --> 01:00:57.530
And it's really
hard on a big panel

01:00:57.530 --> 01:00:58.550
to make sure there's no bubbles.

01:00:58.550 --> 01:01:00.550
But on the smaller ones,
it's mostly the cutting

01:01:00.550 --> 01:01:02.520
that they said they
really need to get rid of.

01:01:02.520 --> 01:01:03.830
So that's what our goal was.

01:01:03.830 --> 01:01:05.390
SID PAL: And the goal,
basically, like Liz said,

01:01:05.390 --> 01:01:06.980
is to eliminate
cutting the cells.

01:01:06.980 --> 01:01:08.210
Because that's a pain.

01:01:08.210 --> 01:01:09.960
LIZ DE REGT: It's a
pain, and they break--

01:01:09.960 --> 01:01:11.750
they said, if they
have five cells,

01:01:11.750 --> 01:01:13.452
they'll break three of them.

01:01:13.452 --> 01:01:15.410
So they won't break these,
because they're just

01:01:15.410 --> 01:01:16.070
full cells.

01:01:19.090 --> 01:01:20.840
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
asked this question,

01:01:20.840 --> 01:01:25.930
but have you tried, in
lab, pointing that halogen

01:01:25.930 --> 01:01:28.000
light at the solar
cell and actually

01:01:28.000 --> 01:01:30.500
fully charging the phone?

01:01:30.500 --> 01:01:31.690
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:01:31.690 --> 01:01:33.790
AUDIENCE: The efficiency
on that is so horrifying.

01:01:33.790 --> 01:01:36.910
Has to be, I mean,
it's like 0.1%,

01:01:36.910 --> 01:01:40.330
I mean, it's like 10
amps, 15 amps at 120 volts

01:01:40.330 --> 01:01:43.300
to get a milliwat in the
phone, which is crazy.

01:01:43.300 --> 01:01:44.700
But you should try it.

01:01:44.700 --> 01:01:46.330
SID PAL: Yeah, we actually got--

01:01:46.330 --> 01:01:48.580
the video we showed
you was yesterday.

01:01:48.580 --> 01:01:51.483
So we're probably going to
try that over the weekend.

01:01:51.483 --> 01:01:52.900
AUDIENCE: You
should just set it--

01:01:52.900 --> 01:01:55.030
I mean, find a phone
that will actually

01:01:55.030 --> 01:01:58.630
give you some kind of decent
readout of how charged it is.

01:01:58.630 --> 01:02:01.390
And run it for half
an hour and see

01:02:01.390 --> 01:02:03.490
how much the charge
increases over half an hour,

01:02:03.490 --> 01:02:06.190
or take out the battery,
measure the voltage on the cell

01:02:06.190 --> 01:02:08.080
at a couple of different
times or something.

01:02:08.080 --> 01:02:10.122
LIZ DE REGT: We did a
rough calculation based on.

01:02:10.122 --> 01:02:12.130
It should have about
100 milliamp current

01:02:12.130 --> 01:02:13.510
and how long that should take.

01:02:13.510 --> 01:02:15.220
And it'll be longer
than the China one.

01:02:15.220 --> 01:02:16.240
The China one was
about five hours.

01:02:16.240 --> 01:02:18.550
And this, hopefully, will be
like seven or eight hours,

01:02:18.550 --> 01:02:20.410
but we have not tested this.

01:02:20.410 --> 01:02:23.227
So that could be a big problem
we have to figure that out.

01:02:23.227 --> 01:02:25.810
AUDIENCE: You may just find that
you need to have the storage.

01:02:25.810 --> 01:02:28.300
There's not enough-- if there's
not enough current coming out

01:02:28.300 --> 01:02:29.860
of that thing to
charge in real time,

01:02:29.860 --> 01:02:31.330
you may have to add
a battery to that,

01:02:31.330 --> 01:02:33.220
so you can store a little
bit of that charge.

01:02:33.220 --> 01:02:34.510
[INAUDIBLE]

01:02:34.510 --> 01:02:36.100
JUAN DIAZ: We have
also been testing

01:02:36.100 --> 01:02:37.990
the voltage
necessary to actually

01:02:37.990 --> 01:02:41.110
get enough voltage to--

01:02:41.110 --> 01:02:42.610
so we can get enough
voltage that it

01:02:42.610 --> 01:02:44.230
can be stepped up to 5 volts.

01:02:44.230 --> 01:02:45.880
And right now, we're
getting that is

01:02:45.880 --> 01:02:47.695
1.2 volts is the minimum.

01:02:47.695 --> 01:02:49.570
According to the circuit,
we should be able--

01:02:49.570 --> 01:02:52.822
we should be able to step up to
5 volts using only 0.7 volts.

01:02:52.822 --> 01:02:54.280
So we're going to
keep trying that.

01:02:54.280 --> 01:02:56.613
LIZ DE REGT: But it also
shouldn't be much of a problem,

01:02:56.613 --> 01:02:58.847
because in Nicaraguan sun,
it is a volt and a half.

01:02:58.847 --> 01:03:00.430
It would have to be
like sunset for it

01:03:00.430 --> 01:03:03.250
to be that low if he's
charging our solar cellphone

01:03:03.250 --> 01:03:04.683
at that hour of the day.

01:03:04.683 --> 01:03:06.850
SID PAL: Yeah, but I mean,
yeah, so we're definitely

01:03:06.850 --> 01:03:09.448
going to try that though just
try and charge the cellphone.

01:03:09.448 --> 01:03:10.990
Part of our initial
plan was actually

01:03:10.990 --> 01:03:15.670
to have a separate energy
storage device in our system.

01:03:15.670 --> 01:03:20.180
But we decided that
would increase costs.

01:03:20.180 --> 01:03:24.280
And so we thought that if
we could do it without that,

01:03:24.280 --> 01:03:28.570
But yeah that's always
a possibility, though.

