PROFESSOR: Feedback from
Mauro or others a GrupoFenix?

01:03:31.047 --> 01:03:33.130
LIZ DE REGT: Yeah, we
really need to talk to them.

01:03:33.130 --> 01:03:35.332
They're not very good
at getting back to us.

01:03:35.332 --> 01:03:36.790
PROFESSOR: OK, so
I would encourage

01:03:36.790 --> 01:03:38.482
you to use the phone next week.

01:03:38.482 --> 01:03:39.940
LIZ DE REGT: Yeah,
we want to talk.

01:03:39.940 --> 01:03:41.110
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:03:41.110 --> 01:03:43.068
We have a lot of questions,
that a conversation

01:03:43.068 --> 01:03:45.640
will be really helpful.

01:03:45.640 --> 01:03:47.470
AUDIENCE: So how
do non-Nokia phones

01:03:47.470 --> 01:03:49.092
did you see when you were there?

01:03:49.092 --> 01:03:51.550
LIZ DE REGT: We only actually
saw two kinds of cell phones,

01:03:51.550 --> 01:03:53.680
we saw Nokia and ZTE phone.

01:03:53.680 --> 01:03:56.740
The ZTE phone can be
charged in similar way.

01:03:56.740 --> 01:04:02.020
It's the same voltage, but
it's a different connector.

01:04:02.020 --> 01:04:04.900
We could also do this
with a USB charger,

01:04:04.900 --> 01:04:07.480
but the Nokia phone
can't be charged via USB.

01:04:07.480 --> 01:04:09.640
So we just used that--

01:04:09.640 --> 01:04:11.470
we're waiting--
Lindsay, the woman,

01:04:11.470 --> 01:04:13.990
who is the liaison that the
other group was talking about,

01:04:13.990 --> 01:04:17.800
said that she has a spreadsheet
with what kind of cell phones

01:04:17.800 --> 01:04:18.700
everyone has.

01:04:18.700 --> 01:04:21.107
We're trying to get that her.

01:04:21.107 --> 01:04:23.440
But we asked a lot of people,
and those are the only two

01:04:23.440 --> 01:04:24.550
cellphones we saw.

01:04:24.550 --> 01:04:26.410
SID PAL: So right now, these
are the two connectors we have.

01:04:26.410 --> 01:04:27.535
This one charges the Nokia.

01:04:27.535 --> 01:04:29.440
And then this one's
a USB charger.

01:04:29.440 --> 01:04:36.390
So any more questions?

01:04:36.390 --> 01:04:38.792
OK, well, thank you.

01:04:38.792 --> 01:04:41.768
[APPLAUSE]

01:04:47.052 --> 01:04:48.510
GUEST SPEAKER 1:
As we speak, we're

01:04:48.510 --> 01:04:52.740
actually going to pass
around the little Ziploc bags

01:04:52.740 --> 01:04:55.200
of the experiments
we did with the rice.

01:04:55.200 --> 01:04:59.610
And the NGO that we worked
with was called ASAPROSAR.

01:04:59.610 --> 01:05:01.740
And we are actually
part of the class

01:05:01.740 --> 01:05:05.790
that went to El Salvador
and not Nicaragua.

01:05:05.790 --> 01:05:10.020
We worked in a community called
La Magdalena in El Salvador.

01:05:10.020 --> 01:05:13.650
And the NGO is currently
introducing rice cultivation

01:05:13.650 --> 01:05:17.910
in the community both to be
used as a source of income

01:05:17.910 --> 01:05:19.710
and also as nutrition.

01:05:19.710 --> 01:05:23.970
And in order for the rice
to be ready for consumption,

01:05:23.970 --> 01:05:25.050
it needs to be processed.

01:05:25.050 --> 01:05:28.260
And the process involves
threshing and dehusking.

01:05:28.260 --> 01:05:34.080
And current methods of dehusking
are very time and money

01:05:34.080 --> 01:05:34.680
intensive.

01:05:34.680 --> 01:05:36.720
They usually involve
a two to three

01:05:36.720 --> 01:05:40.740
hour bus ride to a mill where
the rice can be processed.

01:05:40.740 --> 01:05:43.800
And it costs about $3
to $4, we found out,

01:05:43.800 --> 01:05:47.250
which is about a daily salary.

01:05:47.250 --> 01:05:51.840
So our design challenge is
to develop a method that's--

01:05:51.840 --> 01:05:58.590
for more local, faster, and
cheaper way of dehusking rice.

01:05:58.590 --> 01:06:01.980
The two most important
project specifications

01:06:01.980 --> 01:06:04.240
that we focused on
were the efficiency,

01:06:04.240 --> 01:06:05.970
which refers to
the amount of rice

01:06:05.970 --> 01:06:08.490
that's successfully
dehusked, and also

01:06:08.490 --> 01:06:12.000
how gentle the
process is, meaning

01:06:12.000 --> 01:06:15.210
we wanted to minimize the amount
of rice that's broken or cut

01:06:15.210 --> 01:06:16.080
in half.

01:06:16.080 --> 01:06:18.540
And some of the other
specifications we looked at

01:06:18.540 --> 01:06:24.030
were safety, affordability, of
course, durability, and also

01:06:24.030 --> 01:06:26.860
aesthetics.

01:06:26.860 --> 01:06:30.490
We approached the
problem by brainstorming.

01:06:30.490 --> 01:06:33.220
We researched some
existing technologies

01:06:33.220 --> 01:06:36.370
that people use in regions
that rice is grown.

01:06:36.370 --> 01:06:38.020
Some of them are more simple.

01:06:38.020 --> 01:06:41.350
Some of them involve
heavy machinery.

01:06:41.350 --> 01:06:44.290
What we concluded, though, is
that all of these technologies

01:06:44.290 --> 01:06:47.830
use friction in order to
get the husk from the rice.

01:06:47.830 --> 01:06:51.130
And it's a matter of adjusting
the amount of friction

01:06:51.130 --> 01:06:55.210
we have in order to get the
husk off, but not break the rice

01:06:55.210 --> 01:06:55.900
grain.

01:06:55.900 --> 01:06:58.030
And some of the
ideas we ran through

01:06:58.030 --> 01:07:02.170
were pepper grinder, where
we have cylinders rotating

01:07:02.170 --> 01:07:06.070
together, rubbing
together plates

01:07:06.070 --> 01:07:09.250
that can be made from different
materials, mortar and pestle

01:07:09.250 --> 01:07:13.180
idea which is used, but it's
a lot more energy intensive

01:07:13.180 --> 01:07:16.090
and not as efficient, and
also the molino, which

01:07:16.090 --> 01:07:20.410
is a corn mill that's
currently used in El Salvador

01:07:20.410 --> 01:07:21.550
to grain corn.

01:07:21.550 --> 01:07:24.970
And that's basically
two circular plates

01:07:24.970 --> 01:07:26.920
that rub together.

01:07:26.920 --> 01:07:31.430
And that's what we
actually have over there.

01:07:31.430 --> 01:07:33.050
GUEST SPEAKER 2: So
Amy Smith actually

01:07:33.050 --> 01:07:35.150
had visited this
community before we had.

01:07:35.150 --> 01:07:37.340
And she had looked
at this molino

01:07:37.340 --> 01:07:42.388
and inserted a black inner tube
in between the two metal parts.

01:07:42.388 --> 01:07:44.180
So we use that kind of
as our standard when

01:07:44.180 --> 01:07:46.730
we were looking
at how we could go

01:07:46.730 --> 01:07:49.290
about improving this process.

01:07:49.290 --> 01:07:52.460
So we used this Pugh chart
with these attributes

01:07:52.460 --> 01:07:55.250
to figure out which one would
be the most feasible to do this

01:07:55.250 --> 01:07:58.850
semester and which one
would be the most efficient

01:07:58.850 --> 01:08:02.610
for the community
to use overall.

01:08:02.610 --> 01:08:06.750
In the end, we chose to
modify the existing molino

01:08:06.750 --> 01:08:09.750
by inserting a different
material in between the plates.

01:08:09.750 --> 01:08:13.020
Right now, we have the two
leather inserts in there.

01:08:16.390 --> 01:08:19.510
So we kind of just
add a circular insert

01:08:19.510 --> 01:08:22.060
in between them.

01:08:22.060 --> 01:08:23.590
The advantages of
using the molino

01:08:23.590 --> 01:08:27.310
is that our community
partner, ASAPROSAR is actually

01:08:27.310 --> 01:08:32.109
distributing the molinos to
the community to use for corn--

01:08:32.109 --> 01:08:35.590
to make cornmeal,
to grind yucca, to--

01:08:35.590 --> 01:08:37.990
and so it's very
multifunctional for them.

01:08:37.990 --> 01:08:40.569
And although the cost
is $20, ASAPROSAR

01:08:40.569 --> 01:08:44.050
is funding this distribution
to the communities.

01:08:44.050 --> 01:08:46.725
And as it is now, either
one family will use it,

01:08:46.725 --> 01:08:48.100
or a whole community
will use it.

01:08:48.100 --> 01:08:50.770
So it's available
to a lot of people.

01:08:50.770 --> 01:08:51.850
It's pretty durable.

01:08:51.850 --> 01:08:53.890
The metal is pretty rigid.

01:08:53.890 --> 01:08:55.490
And it's not labor intensive.

01:08:55.490 --> 01:08:58.080
It's just a hand
crank that you use.

01:08:58.080 --> 01:08:59.990
A few of the challenges
with the molino,

01:08:59.990 --> 01:09:03.460
though, is that when you just
do it without any insert,

01:09:03.460 --> 01:09:06.130
it cracks or breaks the rice.

01:09:06.130 --> 01:09:09.729
So people don't really
necessarily want broken rice.

01:09:09.729 --> 01:09:11.229
And it wears the material down.

01:09:11.229 --> 01:09:13.090
If you put something
in there, we kind of

01:09:13.090 --> 01:09:19.040
need a durable material that
will withstand this turning.

01:09:19.040 --> 01:09:21.830
So we decided upon five
different materials

01:09:21.830 --> 01:09:23.810
that we wanted to test.

01:09:23.810 --> 01:09:26.590
And we looked at leather,
rubber, metal, wood,

01:09:26.590 --> 01:09:27.979
and fabric.

01:09:27.979 --> 01:09:31.490
And based on those, we looked at
the cost, residue, durability,

01:09:31.490 --> 01:09:33.109
and availability of those.

01:09:33.109 --> 01:09:36.170
We brought back leather and
rubber from El Salvador,

01:09:36.170 --> 01:09:38.540
so we actually could
use stuff that was

01:09:38.540 --> 01:09:40.220
available in those communities.

01:09:40.220 --> 01:09:43.819
And Lucy will talk to
you about what we found.

01:09:43.819 --> 01:09:46.340
GUEST SPEAKER 3: So we did
quite a few experiments

01:09:46.340 --> 01:09:48.660
where the main variables
that we were really testing

01:09:48.660 --> 01:09:50.960
were what type of material
that we wanted to use,

01:09:50.960 --> 01:09:53.660
and then also how many
turns of the molino

01:09:53.660 --> 01:09:55.890
that we were going to do.

01:09:55.890 --> 01:10:00.830
So first, to just highlight the
main steps of the experiments

01:10:00.830 --> 01:10:03.620
that we did, first, we
inserted the material.

01:10:03.620 --> 01:10:07.400
And then in order to be
able to accurately measure

01:10:07.400 --> 01:10:09.320
how much space there
actually existed

01:10:09.320 --> 01:10:14.030
in between the sides of
the molino and the insert,

01:10:14.030 --> 01:10:15.560
we tightened it
as far as we could

01:10:15.560 --> 01:10:17.870
once we inserted the material.

01:10:17.870 --> 01:10:21.170
And then we measured it from
the number of turns out,

01:10:21.170 --> 01:10:24.380
so how much we
actually loosened it

01:10:24.380 --> 01:10:26.400
from the most tight position.

01:10:26.400 --> 01:10:28.610
And then we use the
same amount of rice

01:10:28.610 --> 01:10:30.500
for each experiment, 50 grams.

01:10:30.500 --> 01:10:34.790
And we ran it through, and once
it had gone through the molino,

01:10:34.790 --> 01:10:38.870
we sifted it through to get rid
of the really crushed pieces.

01:10:38.870 --> 01:10:41.900
And then we winnowed
it, so essentially just

01:10:41.900 --> 01:10:46.210
tossing the rice up, so the air
will catch and blow the husks

01:10:46.210 --> 01:10:46.710
away.

01:10:46.710 --> 01:10:48.140
So you can get rid of the husks.

01:10:48.140 --> 01:10:52.700
And then we separated the
rice into four main components

01:10:52.700 --> 01:10:54.740
being the rice that
was still husked,

01:10:54.740 --> 01:10:56.570
the rice that was
whole, the rice that

01:10:56.570 --> 01:10:59.990
was crushed or broken,
and then also if there

01:10:59.990 --> 01:11:01.710
were any husks left as well.

01:11:01.710 --> 01:11:03.800
And then we noted--

01:11:03.800 --> 01:11:05.270
and you'll see the
bags themselves

01:11:05.270 --> 01:11:08.060
are labeled, as they're
going passing around,

01:11:08.060 --> 01:11:12.470
how many turns that it
took and then the number--

01:11:12.470 --> 01:11:15.410
the weight of the
still husked rice,

01:11:15.410 --> 01:11:18.680
the weight of the
husked rice, and if it

01:11:18.680 --> 01:11:20.370
was in pieces and such.

01:11:20.370 --> 01:11:21.830
So this is a--

01:11:21.830 --> 01:11:22.970
I like this picture--

01:11:22.970 --> 01:11:26.090
is a picture of the rice
that has-- is still husked,

01:11:26.090 --> 01:11:31.040
a whole grain of dehusked
rice, a broken grain, and then

01:11:31.040 --> 01:11:32.540
some of the
particles that we use

01:11:32.540 --> 01:11:35.480
to sift through to get rid of.

01:11:35.480 --> 01:11:38.600
And so one of the experiments
we use with the honeycomb rubber

01:11:38.600 --> 01:11:41.590
is what we called it--

01:11:41.590 --> 01:11:43.400
the picture is kind
of self-explanatory

01:11:43.400 --> 01:11:44.360
why we called it that.

01:11:44.360 --> 01:11:48.320
But so we did a couple
of experiments with this.

01:11:48.320 --> 01:11:53.255
And you can see in the pictures
that we have, whether the--

01:11:53.255 --> 01:11:55.130
it's kind of hard to
see from these pictures,

01:11:55.130 --> 01:12:00.200
but once we've separated it
through how much of the rice

01:12:00.200 --> 01:12:02.060
is still husked,
how much of the rice

01:12:02.060 --> 01:12:05.090
has been dehusked based
on the number of turns.

01:12:05.090 --> 01:12:07.110
What we noted about
the rubber, though,

01:12:07.110 --> 01:12:10.880
is that we weren't very sure
how hazardous the residue that

01:12:10.880 --> 01:12:13.220
might end up on
the rice would be,

01:12:13.220 --> 01:12:16.010
because it's not an
organic material.

01:12:16.010 --> 01:12:20.250
We also noted that this rubber
cost about $6 per square foot.

01:12:20.250 --> 01:12:22.980
So it's not too inexpensive.

01:12:22.980 --> 01:12:25.730
Another, we decide to
flip that rubber around

01:12:25.730 --> 01:12:29.150
and decided to test it with
the different textures.

01:12:29.150 --> 01:12:32.750
And the same thing
happens with this rubber.

01:12:32.750 --> 01:12:34.130
Again, it's not organic.

01:12:34.130 --> 01:12:38.330
So we did a quick
little data sheet

01:12:38.330 --> 01:12:42.080
based on the number of turns
and the mass we started with,

01:12:42.080 --> 01:12:43.670
and then the mass
that was broken,

01:12:43.670 --> 01:12:46.430
and just to get an idea.

01:12:46.430 --> 01:12:49.820
Then we continued on with
leather, which was the kind--

01:12:49.820 --> 01:12:51.920
since it's organic and
a little bit cheaper

01:12:51.920 --> 01:12:54.540
than the rubber, made us
feel a little bit better,

01:12:54.540 --> 01:12:56.540
it's the one we
kind of preferred.

01:12:56.540 --> 01:12:59.210
And it's very readily tested.

01:12:59.210 --> 01:13:02.870
But we were trying to decide
how many layers of the leather

01:13:02.870 --> 01:13:05.330
we should put.

01:13:09.810 --> 01:13:13.510
All right, so as we did the
experiments with a leather,

01:13:13.510 --> 01:13:15.880
sometimes we processed
it through twice.

01:13:15.880 --> 01:13:18.280
Sometimes we did
different number of turns

01:13:18.280 --> 01:13:19.550
with different layers.

01:13:19.550 --> 01:13:22.690
And you can see, most of
them are relatively the same,

01:13:22.690 --> 01:13:24.970
some were a little bit better.

01:13:24.970 --> 01:13:29.390
But ultimately, we decided--

01:13:29.390 --> 01:13:32.580
well, we decided that the
material wasn't the biggest

01:13:32.580 --> 01:13:33.080
part.

01:13:33.080 --> 01:13:36.760
But we still decided to
test with duct tape, which

01:13:36.760 --> 01:13:39.320
we found, unfortunately,
wore away relatively quickly.

01:13:39.320 --> 01:13:42.070
And then we tried for the
heavyweight of a flat sheet

01:13:42.070 --> 01:13:43.100
metal surface.

01:13:43.100 --> 01:13:46.120
And essentially,
that was a disaster.

01:13:46.120 --> 01:13:48.700
So I'll hand it over to Benji.

01:13:48.700 --> 01:13:51.490
GUEST SPEAKER 4: So
one of the reasons

01:13:51.490 --> 01:13:53.330
that we decided to go
against the flat metal

01:13:53.330 --> 01:13:55.090
was because the rice
wasn't actually fed

01:13:55.090 --> 01:13:57.970
into the molino the
way that the plate,

01:13:57.970 --> 01:14:01.000
the way that the backplate
is designed and shape it

01:14:01.000 --> 01:14:01.810
feeds in the rice.

01:14:01.810 --> 01:14:06.005
And so we decided to just
focus on the one side,

01:14:06.005 --> 01:14:07.630
on the back side of
the molino and just

01:14:07.630 --> 01:14:09.230
adding the material there.

01:14:09.230 --> 01:14:12.340
So as you can see, we
have a lot of the tests.

01:14:12.340 --> 01:14:15.130
And we did a lot of
tests, but actually, there

01:14:15.130 --> 01:14:17.710
was no conclusive
awesome material

01:14:17.710 --> 01:14:20.870
that was way better at husking
the rice than the others.

01:14:20.870 --> 01:14:24.610
And so basically we
said, OK, well we

01:14:24.610 --> 01:14:27.490
noticed that the most important
variable for deciding that

01:14:27.490 --> 01:14:30.820
was the number of turns
away from tightening it.

01:14:30.820 --> 01:14:33.460
And so we said, well, we still
have to decide on a material.

01:14:33.460 --> 01:14:38.680
So we wanted to optimize
how much rice is broken,

01:14:38.680 --> 01:14:42.130
and also just think about the
robustness and the availability

01:14:42.130 --> 01:14:44.240
of the material.

01:14:44.240 --> 01:14:46.720
And so we decided
on choosing leather,

01:14:46.720 --> 01:14:48.440
because of the
availability and because

01:14:48.440 --> 01:14:52.300
of-- because it is
organic, it's much safer.

01:14:52.300 --> 01:14:57.220
And so going, we did a couple
of more exploratory tests

01:14:57.220 --> 01:15:00.310
with the leather and trying
to decide how soft we wanted

01:15:00.310 --> 01:15:01.780
to make the leather,
effectively,

01:15:01.780 --> 01:15:03.430
like how it interacts
with the rice.

01:15:03.430 --> 01:15:06.350
And so we tried with two pieces
of leather glued together

01:15:06.350 --> 01:15:07.720
and then three pieces together.

01:15:07.720 --> 01:15:10.990
And unsurprisingly, we
actually found that they

01:15:10.990 --> 01:15:14.080
were very similar as well.

01:15:14.080 --> 01:15:16.300
So we decided on going
with two pieces of leather

01:15:16.300 --> 01:15:18.850
rather than the three, because
it's just less material,

01:15:18.850 --> 01:15:20.565
it's less expensive.

01:15:20.565 --> 01:15:21.940
And so going
forward, we're going

01:15:21.940 --> 01:15:26.110
to be optimizing that
two layer process.

01:15:26.110 --> 01:15:28.870
Or how we're going to be
gluing those together,

01:15:28.870 --> 01:15:30.770
what type of glue,
or if we're going

01:15:30.770 --> 01:15:32.770
to sew them together,
because the glue might not

01:15:32.770 --> 01:15:34.400
be available.

01:15:34.400 --> 01:15:39.310
And so we'll be looking at
those details of that leather

01:15:39.310 --> 01:15:40.360
material.

01:15:40.360 --> 01:15:45.070
But also, we wanted to look at
the process of how it's being--

01:15:45.070 --> 01:15:47.523
how it's being put
through the mill,

01:15:47.523 --> 01:15:48.940
so not only changing
the material,

01:15:48.940 --> 01:15:52.450
but also thinking, well, if we
change the number of turns to,

01:15:52.450 --> 01:15:55.370
say, three times
more than usual.

01:15:55.370 --> 01:15:58.210
And then going through it twice,
does that change the results?

01:15:58.210 --> 01:16:02.110
Is it more gentle
with the rice if you

01:16:02.110 --> 01:16:05.512
process through it twice while
husking more of the material.

01:16:05.512 --> 01:16:07.720
So we'll be looking at that,
or if we can do anything

01:16:07.720 --> 01:16:09.550
to the rice prior to
actually running it

01:16:09.550 --> 01:16:11.860
through the molino that
would make it more effective

01:16:11.860 --> 01:16:13.750
for dehusking it.

01:16:13.750 --> 01:16:14.913
So that's about it.

01:16:14.913 --> 01:16:16.330
If you guys have
any questions, we

01:16:16.330 --> 01:16:19.667
can also show how this
is working so far.

01:16:19.667 --> 01:16:22.152
GUEST SPEAKER 2: One reason
was for explaining it

01:16:22.152 --> 01:16:23.146
to community partners.

01:16:23.146 --> 01:16:26.730
It's a lot easier to
say [INAUDIBLE] tight

01:16:26.730 --> 01:16:28.760
as you can tighten it
as opposed to three

01:16:28.760 --> 01:16:30.450
millimeters in
between, because you

01:16:30.450 --> 01:16:32.408
don't want to use exact
measurements like that.

01:16:32.408 --> 01:16:34.448
And also kind of gave us--

01:16:34.448 --> 01:16:36.698
using different materials
gave us a lot of flexibility

01:16:36.698 --> 01:16:38.688
with [INAUDIBLE].

01:16:42.560 --> 01:16:47.440
AUDIENCE: So this is the
you're talking about.

01:16:47.440 --> 01:16:52.660
So let's see I let you know

01:16:52.660 --> 01:16:55.030
GUEST SPEAKER 1: And then
how many turns do you do?

01:16:55.030 --> 01:16:56.452
GUEST SPEAKER 2: Three.

01:16:56.452 --> 01:17:00.142
GUEST SPEAKER 1:
One, two, three.

01:17:00.142 --> 01:17:02.600
GUEST SPEAKER 4: We could do
a little more, so it's easier.

01:17:09.050 --> 01:17:11.180
GUEST SPEAKER 1: And it
takes a little bit of--

01:17:11.180 --> 01:17:13.640
the first few turns and
the last few hours are--

01:17:17.060 --> 01:17:19.280
but it takes a while
for it to start,

01:17:19.280 --> 01:17:24.200
because it just needs some
amount of rice to be--

01:17:24.200 --> 01:17:26.180
GUEST SPEAKER 2: So we have--

01:17:26.180 --> 01:17:27.918
a little bit of--

01:17:27.918 --> 01:17:29.210
there's a lot of broken pieces.

01:17:29.210 --> 01:17:33.260
So with this setup, we found
that four, or five, or six

01:17:33.260 --> 01:17:38.090
turns, just because we
found that having the rice

01:17:38.090 --> 01:17:41.930
not actually be
husked, we'd rather

01:17:41.930 --> 01:17:44.360
have rice that's not
dehusked as opposed

01:17:44.360 --> 01:17:45.690
to a lot of broken pieces.

01:17:54.127 --> 01:17:56.210
GUEST SPEAKER 4: I'm not
sure if you guys also saw

01:17:56.210 --> 01:17:58.640
the bag that was just broken.

01:17:58.640 --> 01:18:00.480
So basically, it still husks.

01:18:00.480 --> 01:18:02.180
It still husks
[INAUDIBLE] material,

01:18:02.180 --> 01:18:06.170
but it's actually much
more effective than just--

01:18:09.158 --> 01:18:13.150
AUDIENCE: Where's
the leather from?

01:18:13.150 --> 01:18:14.470
GUEST SPEAKER 4: From a shoe?

01:18:14.470 --> 01:18:16.430
AUDIENCE: Do you know
how they made it?

01:18:16.430 --> 01:18:18.380
This is leather tanning,
I mean, that leather

01:18:18.380 --> 01:18:21.105
could be the most toxic
thing you have, conceivably.

01:18:21.105 --> 01:18:23.480
I mean, one of the things that
is used in leather tanning

01:18:23.480 --> 01:18:26.690
is chromium sulfate
as the tanning agent.

01:18:26.690 --> 01:18:28.310
So like it's just
worth finding out.

01:18:28.310 --> 01:18:30.290
I mean, another
option is tannin,

01:18:30.290 --> 01:18:32.330
which comes from oak trees.

01:18:32.330 --> 01:18:36.232
Guessing not a lot of oak tress
in El Salvador, but, yeah,

01:18:36.232 --> 01:18:37.190
it's worth finding out.

01:18:41.320 --> 01:18:45.760
Because it would be sad
to poison everybody.

01:18:45.760 --> 01:18:48.010
It's hard to get invited
back after-- well,

01:18:48.010 --> 01:18:50.480
they can't invite you
back if they're all dead.

01:18:50.480 --> 01:18:53.410
GUEST SPEAKER 1: Also,
we've been waiting for them

01:18:53.410 --> 01:18:57.550
to respond to questions.

01:18:57.550 --> 01:19:00.070
But some of the
questions that we've

01:19:00.070 --> 01:19:03.940
been trying to get answers
to are how many people

01:19:03.940 --> 01:19:05.485
may need the--

01:19:05.485 --> 01:19:07.510
how many people can
use it, would it

01:19:07.510 --> 01:19:09.070
be a family or whole community?

01:19:09.070 --> 01:19:10.720
And how much rice
do they consume?

01:19:10.720 --> 01:19:12.510
How much rice can they grow?

01:19:12.510 --> 01:19:15.010
And I think that's going-- it's
going to be important for us

01:19:15.010 --> 01:19:18.280
to in terms of the material and
how durable the material is.

01:19:18.280 --> 01:19:21.850
And is it-- does each family
have a piece of this leather,

01:19:21.850 --> 01:19:25.210
or is it permanently
attached to the molino.

01:19:25.210 --> 01:19:27.662
Or so there's a lot of
questions that we're still--

01:19:27.662 --> 01:19:29.620
AUDIENCE: I just wouldn't
take for granted what

01:19:29.620 --> 01:19:31.980
is and isn't toxic based on.

01:19:31.980 --> 01:19:34.450
Because the rubber could be
fine for all that we know.

01:19:34.450 --> 01:19:38.410
And the leather
could be poisonous.

01:19:38.410 --> 01:19:41.260
And if what comes off is
stuff that you can wash off,

01:19:41.260 --> 01:19:42.520
then it's not--

01:19:42.520 --> 01:19:43.000
GUEST SPEAKER 2:
Well, the problem

01:19:43.000 --> 01:19:44.708
is the inner tube was
that it was leaving

01:19:44.708 --> 01:19:46.150
like a black residue on there.

01:19:46.150 --> 01:19:47.650
AUDIENCE: Yeah, if
it's like marking

01:19:47.650 --> 01:19:49.570
like a crayon on each one, yeah.

01:19:49.570 --> 01:19:51.760
But if there's a little
bit of rubber dust

01:19:51.760 --> 01:19:53.840
that comes off, but is
not adhering to anything,

01:19:53.840 --> 01:19:55.070
you can wash it off--

01:19:55.070 --> 01:19:57.370
AUDIENCE: Your
[? Yellow ?] [? River. ?]

01:19:57.370 --> 01:19:59.440
AUDIENCE: So it's just
worth actually finding out

01:19:59.440 --> 01:20:01.810
what chemicals are
actually present,

01:20:01.810 --> 01:20:04.810
because it might be that you
could eat a loaf of that rubber

01:20:04.810 --> 01:20:05.810
and you be fine.

01:20:05.810 --> 01:20:07.810
But if you chew on the
leather for a little bit.

01:20:07.810 --> 01:20:08.830
GUEST SPEAKER 4: It's
just hard to control

01:20:08.830 --> 01:20:10.630
that, you know what I mean?

01:20:10.630 --> 01:20:13.900
The shops will be
doing [INAUDIBLE]..

01:20:13.900 --> 01:20:16.670
PROFESSOR: Well, I think
it's worth figuring out.

01:20:16.670 --> 01:20:18.640
Worst case scenario,
this leather

01:20:18.640 --> 01:20:22.360
is tanned with whatever
chemical Steve just mentioned.

01:20:22.360 --> 01:20:24.490
Is that something that's
going to get absorbed

01:20:24.490 --> 01:20:26.823
into the rice or something
that's going to be washed off

01:20:26.823 --> 01:20:27.725
during processing.

01:20:27.725 --> 01:20:29.350
If it's going to get
absorbed, maybe we

01:20:29.350 --> 01:20:31.660
need to knock this
leather out as a contender

01:20:31.660 --> 01:20:32.910
to find something similar.

01:20:32.910 --> 01:20:34.660
Because it seemed like
different materials

01:20:34.660 --> 01:20:36.500
all seem to work
basically the same.

01:20:36.500 --> 01:20:40.210
And so would an old t-shirt
work just as well or something,

01:20:40.210 --> 01:20:41.110
I don't?

01:20:41.110 --> 01:20:42.370
AUDIENCE: How did fabric work?

01:20:42.370 --> 01:20:44.162
GUEST SPEAKER 4: Yeah,
so that's the reason

01:20:44.162 --> 01:20:47.500
we did duct tape was
not as an actual design,

01:20:47.500 --> 01:20:49.240
but just to see
how long it lasts,

01:20:49.240 --> 01:20:51.243
because it is like
generally wear resistant.

01:20:51.243 --> 01:20:52.660
GUEST SPEAKER 1:
But it does wear.

01:20:52.660 --> 01:20:57.010
And we tried this foam material.

01:20:57.010 --> 01:21:00.903
And that-- you can see that--

01:21:00.903 --> 01:21:02.320
GUEST SPEAKER 4:
With even running

01:21:02.320 --> 01:21:04.195
this much of the 50
grams with the duct tape,

01:21:04.195 --> 01:21:05.530
you could tell it was worn.

01:21:05.530 --> 01:21:10.000
And actually do tests
between denim and leather

01:21:10.000 --> 01:21:10.870
for motorcycles.

01:21:10.870 --> 01:21:13.960
And they'd simulate
someone falling down,

01:21:13.960 --> 01:21:16.480
and the leather lasts
for 90 feet of dragging

01:21:16.480 --> 01:21:19.120
while denim lasts for five.

01:21:19.120 --> 01:21:21.577
And denim is like very durable.

01:21:21.577 --> 01:21:23.410
GUEST SPEAKER 2: I don't
if Benji showed you

01:21:23.410 --> 01:21:26.460
about the inside of these.

01:21:26.460 --> 01:21:28.795
Can you--

01:21:28.795 --> 01:21:31.410
AUDIENCE: It was like
trying to grind sandpaper.

01:21:31.410 --> 01:21:32.600
It's going to wreck
whatever you put into it.

01:21:32.600 --> 01:21:33.980
GUEST SPEAKER 2:
Both sides are rich.

01:21:33.980 --> 01:21:35.330
AUDIENCE: There's a reason
why those things are made out

01:21:35.330 --> 01:21:35.830
of metal.

01:21:37.490 --> 01:21:38.990
GUEST SPEAKER 4:
So that's how we're

01:21:38.990 --> 01:21:42.380
looking at doing flat metal,
so that was more durable.

01:21:42.380 --> 01:21:47.910
But like I mentioned, we need
the design on the inner part

01:21:47.910 --> 01:21:49.440
to pull the right through.

01:21:49.440 --> 01:21:53.340
Otherwise, the
material [INAUDIBLE]..

01:21:53.340 --> 01:21:56.870
AUDIENCE: I mean, even if you
make the inner hole, the metal

01:21:56.870 --> 01:21:59.360
piece that goes
on this side, did

01:21:59.360 --> 01:22:03.470
you make the inner hole big
enough so that the rice could

01:22:03.470 --> 01:22:03.980
come out?

01:22:03.980 --> 01:22:06.170
GUEST SPEAKER 4: Yeah,
so it needs the like--

01:22:06.170 --> 01:22:08.990
this cutting in the
other dimension,

01:22:08.990 --> 01:22:11.120
it can't actually just be
a flat surface, no matter

01:22:11.120 --> 01:22:13.520
how big the hole is,
because the rice isn't

01:22:13.520 --> 01:22:15.890
allowed to feed into it.

01:22:15.890 --> 01:22:18.460
GUEST SPEAKER 2: These will
just wear the material down.

01:22:18.460 --> 01:22:19.640
It's on both size.

01:22:19.640 --> 01:22:20.945
AUDIENCE: Have you found--

01:22:20.945 --> 01:22:24.050
I didn't go look, I was too busy
looking up how you tan leather

01:22:24.050 --> 01:22:26.090
while you were
talking about that.

01:22:26.090 --> 01:22:29.300
But there are--
all over YouTube,

01:22:29.300 --> 01:22:32.630
there are videos of how
manufacturing processes happen,

01:22:32.630 --> 01:22:35.390
usually put up by the people
who built the equipment.

01:22:35.390 --> 01:22:38.480
And I'd be curious how they
actually-- how rice actually

01:22:38.480 --> 01:22:39.030
processed.

01:22:39.030 --> 01:22:40.490
GUEST SPEAKER 4:
Yes, they do it--

01:22:40.490 --> 01:22:44.190
they do it with like rollers,
basically, like rubber rollers

01:22:44.190 --> 01:22:47.640
a lot of times, which is-- it's
a totally different geometry

01:22:47.640 --> 01:22:50.010
than the molino.

01:22:50.010 --> 01:22:51.973
But like we said,
it's the friction.

01:22:51.973 --> 01:22:53.640
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I
just wonder if there's

01:22:53.640 --> 01:22:56.670
pieces of that you can steal.

01:22:56.670 --> 01:23:00.720
If that's too coarse, but a flat
plate with a single slot cut in

01:23:00.720 --> 01:23:02.850
would get would you--

01:23:02.850 --> 01:23:04.590
if you had grooves
cut in it that

01:23:04.590 --> 01:23:06.882
happened to be about the
right size for a grain of rice

01:23:06.882 --> 01:23:09.050
or something.

01:23:09.050 --> 01:23:10.500
[INAUDIBLE] I don't know.

01:23:10.500 --> 01:23:12.125
I'm just wondering
if there are tricks.

01:23:12.125 --> 01:23:13.667
PROFESSOR: This
might be too far out,

01:23:13.667 --> 01:23:15.890
but what about a
tumbling process instead

01:23:15.890 --> 01:23:17.300
of a grinding process?

01:23:17.300 --> 01:23:19.490
GUEST SPEAKER 4: Yeah,
the [INAUDIBLE]----

01:23:19.490 --> 01:23:23.810
there's a team also doing
[INAUDIBLE] in the D-Lab

01:23:23.810 --> 01:23:25.040
design, I believe.

01:23:25.040 --> 01:23:26.630
But [INAUDIBLE] is
actually very soft.

01:23:26.630 --> 01:23:29.420
And it doesn't end up getting
damaged by the tumblers.

01:23:29.420 --> 01:23:32.420
Whereas the husks are
actually pretty soft

01:23:32.420 --> 01:23:33.810
and the rice is hard.

01:23:33.810 --> 01:23:37.280
So if there were tumblers,
just from looking at it,

01:23:37.280 --> 01:23:40.280
the rice would probably
get more crushed,

01:23:40.280 --> 01:23:44.867
whereas the husks probably
wouldn't really as much.

01:23:44.867 --> 01:23:47.200
GUEST SPEAKER 2: We found the
soft materials really give

01:23:47.200 --> 01:23:49.283
and allow the husks to
come off with the friction,

01:23:49.283 --> 01:23:51.600
but not damage the rice as much.

01:23:51.600 --> 01:23:56.070
[INAUDIBLE] or softer as
opposed to kind of that.

01:23:56.070 --> 01:23:58.203
AUDIENCE: What's wrong
with broken rice?

01:23:58.203 --> 01:24:00.370
GUEST SPEAKER 3: That's
something that we actually--

01:24:00.370 --> 01:24:03.230
that we wanted to
ask the community.

01:24:03.230 --> 01:24:05.682
GUEST SPEAKER 1: It
kind of gets mushy.

01:24:05.682 --> 01:24:07.390
GUEST SPEAKER 2: Well,
part of the reason

01:24:07.390 --> 01:24:11.350
they want to use this as a
tool in their community is they

01:24:11.350 --> 01:24:13.320
want to sell rice, too.

01:24:13.320 --> 01:24:17.158
I assume, we haven't been in
contact with our community,

01:24:17.158 --> 01:24:18.450
they haven't gotten back to us.

01:24:18.450 --> 01:24:22.623
But we would assume that
they'd want to sell the grains.

01:24:22.623 --> 01:24:25.040
PROFESSOR: One question, I
think I mentioned the last time

01:24:25.040 --> 01:24:29.150
[INAUDIBLE] I want to ask is
what their expectations are

01:24:29.150 --> 01:24:32.450
for broken rice if they
are any, from the mill,

01:24:32.450 --> 01:24:35.660
if they get 20% broken rice
already and they just--

01:24:38.170 --> 01:24:41.780
so I would get on the
phone the next week

01:24:41.780 --> 01:24:45.250
and hopefully reach them.

01:24:45.250 --> 01:24:46.840
Whatever material
you end up with,

01:24:46.840 --> 01:24:49.780
whether it's leather or
something else, figuring out

01:24:49.780 --> 01:24:53.650
just how long it does last, so
how many grams you can process

01:24:53.650 --> 01:24:57.440
before you need to replace it
and then getting like a both

01:24:57.440 --> 01:24:58.900
for your users,
so that they know

01:24:58.900 --> 01:25:01.450
you should be reprocessing,
replacing it this often.

01:25:01.450 --> 01:25:04.990
And also so you can get a cost
estimate for we're going to--

01:25:04.990 --> 01:25:07.538
in a year, this
many tons of rice,

01:25:07.538 --> 01:25:09.580
we're going to need this
much leather or whatever

01:25:09.580 --> 01:25:11.520
it is could be useful.

01:25:15.540 --> 01:25:17.940
AUDIENCE: Did you bring the
rice back from El Salvador?

01:25:17.940 --> 01:25:20.460
Or is it from a farm nearby?

01:25:20.460 --> 01:25:23.640
GUEST SPEAKER 4: It is
from a farm in Texas.

01:25:23.640 --> 01:25:26.670
I mean, from comparison--

01:25:26.670 --> 01:25:29.170
AUDIENCE: Nobody's going to let
you bring a sack of unhusked

01:25:29.170 --> 01:25:31.130
rice from El Salvador.

01:25:31.130 --> 01:25:34.430
If you did, you had it
hidden in a balled up sock.

01:25:34.430 --> 01:25:36.730
PROFESSOR: All right,
thanks, you guys.

